var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-Reference","page":"API Reference","text":"Symbols are accessed via fully-qualified submodule paths (the package does not re-export names into its top-level namespace); using ScatteringTransforms: ScatteringTransforms as ST, then e.g. ST.ScatteringCore.ScatteringTransform(...).","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Grid-support-matrix","page":"API Reference","text":"Planar (Cartesian) and spherical scattering, on uniform/structured and nonuniform/scattered sampling:\n\ndomain uniform / structured nonuniform / scattered\nCartesian ScatteringTransform{D} (FFT/direct sum; GPU via GPUBackend) scattered_planar_scattering (exact direct NUDFT; NonuniformFFTs or FINUFFT fast path)\nSphere (S²) structured_spherical_scattering (exact direct SHT; FastSphericalHarmonics fast path) spherical_scattering (exact direct SHT; NUFSHT fast path)\n\nEvery cell has an in-core, dependency-free default (direct summation), with an optional fast path selected by the transform keyword, which takes a SpectralBackends.jl tag. DirectSumSpectralBackend is the in-core default everywhere; the fast paths are FFTSpectralBackend (FFTW) on a grid, FlowTransformBindings.jl's NonuniformFFTsBackend() or FINUFFTBackend() for scattered points, NUFSHTSpectralBackend (NUFSHT) for the scattered sphere, and FSHTSpectralBackend (FastSphericalHarmonics) for the structured sphere. AutoSpectralBackend (the default) picks the fast path if its extension is loaded, else the direct sum — so nothing requires an external library. Naming a backend explicitly is honoured exactly: if its extension is absent it throws.\n\nMonogenic variants support gridded Cartesian fields and both spherical samplings. spherical_monogenic_components accepts scattered and structured spherical transforms.","title":"Grid-support matrix"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Transforms","page":"API Reference","text":"All transform objects subtype ScatteringCore.AbstractScatteringTransform and are callable as st(field). Both Cartesian families carry spatial rank as ScatteringTransform{D,…} and MonogenicScatteringTransform{D,…}. D = 1, 2, 3 selects a signal, image or volume.","title":"Transforms"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Reconstruction-and-synthesis","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Reconstruction & synthesis"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Monogenic-(Riesz)-scattering","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Monogenic (Riesz) scattering"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Localized-(Mallat)-field","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Localized (Mallat) field"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Coefficients-and-reductions","page":"API Reference","text":"flat_length's docstring also covers the row accessors flat_row_s0, flat_row_s1 and flat_row_s2, which are the layout flatten1d!, flatten2d! and the batched paths all walk.","title":"Coefficients & reductions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Batching-and-backends","page":"API Reference","text":"Where a transform runs is chosen with a backend from ComputationalBackends.jl — SerialBackend, ThreadedBackend, GPUBackend, DistributedBackend, MPIBackend, AutoBackend — passed as execution= to scattering_batch and scattering_batch!; the gridded scattering_transform! entries take SerialBackend or ThreadedBackend the same way. Which spectral algorithm it uses is chosen with a SpectralBackends.jl tag passed as transform= at construction. Each is honoured exactly: naming a backend whose extension is not loaded throws.","title":"Batching & backends"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#The-periodized-cascade","page":"API Reference","text":"Each wavelet's output is produced directly on the grid its band needs, decimated by r = 2^max(j-oversampling, 0) per axis. Exact at oversampling ≥ J, which reproduces the undecimated cascade bit for bit; below that it trades accuracy for speed.","title":"The periodized cascade"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Filter-banks,-filters-and-path-graph","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Filter banks, filters & path graph"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Spectral-plans-and-core-operations","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Spectral plans & core operations"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Scattered-least-squares-solve","page":"API Reference","text":"analysis = ST.Plans.LeastSquares() fits spectral coefficients with FlowTransformBindings.lsmr!. ST.Plans.WeightedAdjoint() applies the quadrature-weighted adjoint. The planar default is WeightedAdjoint(); the scattered-sphere default is LeastSquares().\n\nrtol controls the fit's relative normal residual. A fit that reaches its iteration or condition limit returns its best iterate. Fit diagnostics remain in its LSMR workspace; coefficient containers hold the scattering coefficients. For scattered planar transforms, tol controls NUFFT accuracy and nthreads controls the NUFFT library's threads.","title":"Scattered least-squares solve"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Plans-supplied-by-extensions","page":"API Reference","text":"These are declared in the core and given a method by the corresponding extension. Calling one without its package loaded raises with the using line to run.","title":"Plans supplied by extensions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Execution-backends","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Execution backends"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Cascade-internals","page":"API Reference","text":"The cascade each gridded transform runs, and the per-surface in-place entry points.","title":"Cascade internals"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Spherical-scattering","page":"API Reference","text":"A spherical plan implements three primitives — analysis, multiply-and-synthesise, and the spherical mean — and everything above them is shared. A scattered transform analyses its samples once and runs the rest of its cascade on a plan of the same kind on the Gauss–Legendre grid of its band limit. The in-core direct plan is always available; NUFSHT and FastSphericalHarmonics supply the fast ones.","title":"Spherical scattering"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Plotting","page":"API Reference","text":"Methods are supplied by the CairoMakie extension.","title":"Plotting"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringTransforms","page":"API Reference","text":"ScatteringTransforms.jl — Native Julia implementation of wavelet scattering transforms\n\nSurfaces: 1D signals, 2D and 3D gridded fields, scattered planar points (NUFFT), and the sphere — both on a structured grid and at scattered points. Each has a monogenic variant; the gridded ones also have a localized (Mallat) field output and a multi-resolution second order. The spherical and scattered paths run dependency-free by default, with fast paths supplied by extensions.\n\nQuick Start\n\nNames live in submodules and are reached by their full path; alias the package for brevity.\n\nusing ScatteringTransforms: ScatteringTransforms as ST\n\n# 1D scattering (N = signal length, J = number of octaves)\nsignal = randn(1024)\nst = ST.ScatteringCore.ScatteringTransform(1024, 8; Q=1, max_order=2)\ncoeffs = st(signal)\n\n# 2D planar scattering (N = image size, J = scales, L = orientations)\nimage = randn(256, 256)\nst2d = ST.ScatteringCore.ScatteringTransform((256, 256), 4; L=8, max_order=2)\ncoeffs2d = st2d(image)\n\nReferences\n\nMallat (2012): Group invariant scattering. Comm. Pure Appl. Math.\nBruna & Mallat (2013): Invariant Scattering Convolution Networks. IEEE PAMI.\nCheng & Ménard (2021): How to quantify fields or textures? A guide to the scattering transform.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringTransforms"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.AbstractScatteringTransform","page":"API Reference","text":"AbstractScatteringTransform\n\nPrepared scattering transform whose call st(field) returns scattering coefficients.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.AbstractScatteringTransform"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.ScatteringTransform","page":"API Reference","text":"ScatteringTransform{D}\nScatteringTransform([T=Float64,] dims, J; kwargs...)\n\nRegular Cartesian scattering transform with D spatial axes, a wavelet bank and reusable buffers. The rank of the dims tuple selects the 1D, 2D or 3D wavelet builder; an integer selects 1D.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.ScatteringTransform"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Scattering1D.scattering_transform!","page":"API Reference","text":"scattering_transform!(coeffs, st, signal; execution = SerialBackend()) -> coeffs′\n\nFill coeffs.S1/coeffs.S2 and return the coefficients with S0 set by Coefficients.update_S0, which allocates a new container only when S0 is a scalar.\n\nexecution is where the cascade runs: SerialBackend in this task; ThreadedBackend (OhMyThreads extension) runs the first-order wavelet groups as parallel tasks after one serial input transform.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Scattering1D.scattering_transform!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Scattering2D.scattering_transform2d!","page":"API Reference","text":"scattering_transform2d!(coeffs, st, image; execution = SerialBackend()) -> coeffs′\n\nFill coeffs.S1/coeffs.S2 and return the coefficients with S0 set by Coefficients.update_S0.\n\nexecution is where the cascade runs: SerialBackend in this task; ThreadedBackend (OhMyThreads extension) runs the first-order wavelet groups as parallel tasks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Scattering2D.scattering_transform2d!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Scattering3D.scattering_transform3d!","page":"API Reference","text":"scattering_transform3d!(coeffs, st, volume; execution = SerialBackend()) -> coeffs\n\nIn-place 3D volumetric scattering transform; fills coeffs (a scales×orientations container) and returns it with S0 updated. execution is as for Scattering2D.scattering_transform2d!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Scattering3D.scattering_transform3d!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.scattering","page":"API Reference","text":"scattering(st, x) -> coefficient container\n\nThe coefficients of st(x), computed without mutation so the forward map can be differentiated (as synthesize does) and takes any element type, such as ForwardDiff.Dual.\n\nEvery intermediate comes from the non-mutating Plans.forward_transform and Plans.inverse_transform and broadcasts, so each call allocates; st(x) is the in-place forward pass. Methods are defined for the 1D, 2D and 3D transforms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.scattering"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.scattered_planar_scattering","page":"API Reference","text":"scattered_planar_scattering([T=Float64,] x, y, ms, J; period, origin=(0, 0), L=8, max_order=2,\n                            transform=SpectralBackends.AutoSpectralBackend(),\n                            analysis=Plans.WeightedAdjoint(), weights=nothing, tol=nothing,\n                            maxiter=100, rtol=Plans.default_solver_rtol(T, transform, tol), damp=0,\n                            ntrans=1, nthreads=0, filters=StoredFilters(), oversampling=J)\nscattered_planar_scattering([T,] grid, ms, J; kwargs...)\n\nBuild a 2D planar scattering transform for a scalar field sampled at scattered points (x, y). A nonuniform DFT analyses the samples onto a uniform Fourier mode grid of size ms = (m1, m2), and the cascade of the gridded ScatteringCore.ScatteringTransform{2}, with its oriented Morlet bank and oversampling, runs on that grid: each coefficient is the domain mean of a modulus of the field the analysis recovers. Apply it to a length-M vector of samples.\n\nperiod is the length of the domain the points sample along each axis and origin its lower corner (a number for both axes, or a 2-tuple): mode k has wavelength period/k, so the wavelets' physical scales are those of the domain, and point sets drawn from one domain share them. Points 0:m-1 with period = m are the nodes of the length-m DFT. With FlowGeometries loaded, a 2-D Cartesian FlowGeometries.Grids grid stands in for the points: its active cells are the points, its periodic axes supply their periods, bounded axes their extents plus the coarsest wavelet width, the lowest cell centre supplies the origin, and cell measures supply default weights.\n\ntransform names the nonuniform transform: SpectralBackends.DirectSumSpectralBackend is the in-core, dependency-free exact NUDFT (always available, O(M·prod(ms))); FlowTransformBindings.NonuniformFFTsBackend() and FlowTransformBindings.FINUFFTBackend() select a nonuniform-FFT library (using NonuniformFFTs / using FINUFFT); and SpectralBackends.AutoSpectralBackend (the default) takes NonuniformFFTs when it is loaded, FINUFFT when only it is, and the direct sum otherwise. analysis=Plans.WeightedAdjoint() takes the adjoint weighted by weights, the quadrature of each Fourier coefficient's domain integral: exact on the nodes of the DFT, an estimate elsewhere. analysis=Plans.LeastSquares() fits the band-limited coefficients by least squares (slower, for irregular sampling). weights (length M, normalized to sum to 1) are uniform by default; S0 is the mean of the analysed field. tol is the NUFFT tolerance (ignored by the exact direct sum). ntrans = B builds a transform that runs B fields per call through one execution per cascade step (see scattering_batch). filters = FilterBanks.ComputedFilters() evaluates the wavelets on demand.\n\nThe solve is LSMR (FlowTransformBindings.lsmr!), which costs one type-2 and one type-1 per iteration and keeps both ‖b - Af‖ and ‖A†(b - Af)‖ monotone, so stopping at maxiter returns the best iterate reached. rtol targets ‖A†(b - Af)‖ ≤ rtol ‖A†b‖ and defaults to the accuracy of the transform under it: sqrt(eps(T)) for the exact direct sum and at least 10·tol for a fast library, whose type-1 and type-2 are adjoints only to its own tolerance (Plans.default_solver_rtol). damp is a Tikhonov λ minimising ‖Af - b‖² + λ²‖f‖², 0 by default; with prod(ms) > M the problem is underdetermined, construction warns, and LSMR returns the minimum-norm solution unless a λ is given.\n\nThe Krylov process terminates within prod(ms) steps, and maxiter caps it earlier. A truncated solve returns the best iterate reached but is reproducible only to the accuracy of the transform under it: a multi-threaded NUFFT accumulates its spreading in a run-dependent order, which an unconverged solve amplifies by about cond(A)². Raise maxiter until the solve converges for an answer independent of the thread count, or set nthreads = 1 for bitwise-identical repeats.\n\nnthreads is the fast library's own thread count, honoured by every copy of the plan, including the per-task copies a threaded backend builds. 0 (the default) takes Threads.nthreads() on a serial backend and gives each per-task copy one thread. 1 keeps the library single-threaded everywhere, and its plan executes without allocating; a multi-threaded FFTW plan runs its parallel loop on Julia tasks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.scattered_planar_scattering"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.spherical_scattering","page":"API Reference","text":"spherical_scattering(pts_theta, pts_phi, lmax, J; max_order=2,\n                     transform=SpectralBackends.AutoSpectralBackend(), analysis=Plans.LeastSquares(),\n                     rtol=SphericalCore.default_rtol(transform), maxiter=500, weights=nothing,\n                     ntrans=1, nufft=SpectralBackends.AutoSpectralBackend(), grid_lmax=2lmax)\n\nBuild a spherical scattering transform for a scalar field at scattered points (θ, φ) on S², using smooth difference-of-Gaussians band-pass wavelets. transform names the spherical-harmonic transform: SpectralBackends.DirectSumSpectralBackend is the in-core, dependency-free exact least-squares transform (always available, O(M·(lmax+1)²)); SpectralBackends.NUFSHTSpectralBackend uses the NUFSHT fast path (needs using NUFSHT); SpectralBackends.AutoSpectralBackend (the default) picks NUFSHT if its extension is loaded, else the direct transform. Accurate analysis needs the sampling to resolve the band limit, i.e. roughly M ≳ (lmax+1)² well-distributed points.\n\nThe samples are analysed once, onto the harmonics of degree ≤ lmax. Every band, modulus, mean and second analysis after that runs on the Gauss–Legendre grid of band limit grid_lmax (SphericalCore.gauss_legendre_grid): each coefficient is the spherical mean of a modulus of the field the analysis recovers, and S0 is that field's mean. A modulus is not band limited, and the grid's analysis of it onto degree ≤ lmax is exact for its content up to degree 2grid_lmax + 1 − lmax.\n\nanalysis = Plans.LeastSquares() fits by LSMR, configured by rtol and maxiter, and returns its best iterate at a stopping limit. Plans.WeightedAdjoint() takes the adjoint weighted by weights: the expansion of a degree-lmax field where the weights integrate the product of two degree-lmax harmonics exactly, and an approximation elsewhere. weights (length M, normalized to sum to 1) are the quadrature of that adjoint and the weights of the fit, uniform by default. ntrans = B builds a plan that transforms B co-located fields per call. nufft names the NUFFT library NUFSHT runs on; the in-core plan runs none and accepts only an Auto nufft.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.spherical_scattering"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.structured_spherical_scattering","page":"API Reference","text":"structured_spherical_scattering([T=Float64,] lmax, J; max_order=2,\n                                transform=SpectralBackends.AutoSpectralBackend(),\n                                band_headroom=nothing, rtol=1e-8, maxiter=500)\n\nBuild a spherical scattering transform for a scalar field sampled on the structured equiangular grid (Nθ = lmax+1 colatitudes, Nφ = 2lmax+1 longitudes), using the same smooth difference-of-Gaussians band-pass wavelets as spherical_scattering. Apply it to a (Nθ, Nφ) grid of samples; obtain the grid points with structured_sphere_points. transform names the spherical-harmonic transform: SpectralBackends.DirectSumSpectralBackend (in-core, dependency-free), or SpectralBackends.FSHTSpectralBackend (the fast exact SHT, needs using FastSphericalHarmonics); SpectralBackends.AutoSpectralBackend picks the fast path if its extension is loaded, else the direct SHT.\n\nOn a backend that can narrow its band limit (FastSphericalHarmonics), a finite band_headroom synthesises band j at band limit band_headroom·ℓ_j, discarding the tail SphericalCore.band_lmax describes; the default nothing runs the full-band-limit cascade bit for bit. rtol and maxiter configure the in-core plan's analysis solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.structured_spherical_scattering"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.structured_sphere_points","page":"API Reference","text":"structured_sphere_points(lmax) -> (Θ, Φ)\n\nColatitudes Θ (length lmax+1) and longitudes Φ (length 2lmax+1) of the equiangular structured grid used by structured_spherical_scattering; sample a field as [f(θ, φ) for θ in Θ, φ in Φ]. In-core (no dependency); matches FastSphericalHarmonics.sph_points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.structured_sphere_points"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Inverse.ReconstructionWorkspace","page":"API Reference","text":"ReconstructionWorkspace(st)\n\nScratch for the linear wavelet inverse and for phase retrieval: the complex wavelet coefficient fields (nw of them), the low-pass field, a spectrum accumulator, and the bank's Littlewood–Paley sum frame. Every filter's Fourier response is real, so the dual filters are ψ̂_λ/A and φ̂/A.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Inverse.ReconstructionWorkspace"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Inverse.wavelet_transform!","page":"API Reference","text":"wavelet_transform(st, x) -> (; wavelet, lowpass)\nwavelet_transform!(ws, st, x) -> (; wavelet, lowpass)\n\nThe linear (pre-modulus) wavelet layer underlying the scattering transform: the complex wavelet coefficient fields wavelet[λ] = x ⋆ ψ_λ and the low-pass field lowpass = x ⋆ φ, which together determine x exactly (see iwavelet!). The ! form returns arrays owned by ws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Inverse.wavelet_transform!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Inverse.iwavelet!","page":"API Reference","text":"iwavelet(st, wavelet, lowpass) -> x\niwavelet(st, wt) -> x\niwavelet!(ws, st, wavelet, lowpass) -> x\n\nInverse of wavelet_transform! for a real field by the canonical dual frame, x = Re F⁻¹[(φ̂·Ŷ + Σ_λ ψ̂_λ·Ŵ_λ) / A] with A the bank's Littlewood–Paley sum: the real part takes C(k) to (C(k) + conj C(−k))/2, which turns C = (φ̂² + Σ_λ ψ̂_λ²)·x̂ into A·x̂. Returns the reconstructed real field; iwavelet(st, wavelet_transform(st, x)) ≈ x to rounding.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Inverse.iwavelet!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Inverse.reconstruct_phase","page":"API Reference","text":"reconstruct_phase(st, moduli; iters=200, init=nothing, seed_lowpass=nothing) -> x\n\nPhase retrieval from the first-order moduli moduli[λ] = |x ⋆ ψ_λ| (the real fields the scattering transform averages), via Gerchberg–Saxton alternating projections:\n\ntake the linear wavelet transform of the current estimate;\nre-impose the target magnitudes on each band-pass channel (keeping the recovered phase);\nreconstruct with the exact frame inverse iwavelet!; repeat.\n\nThe low-pass channel carries no magnitude target, so it is taken from the current estimate each iteration (or from seed_lowpass if supplied). The reconstruction is determined only up to a global sign/phase. init (defaults to a random field matched in energy to the moduli) seeds the estimate; pass one for reproducibility. Returns the real reconstructed field, on the array type of the transform's filters. The loop reuses one workspace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Inverse.reconstruct_phase"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.synthesize","page":"API Reference","text":"synthesize(st, target; backend, init=nothing, iters=500, lr=0.05, loss=scattering_loss) -> (; field, losses)\n\nReconstruct a field whose scattering coefficients match target by gradient descent (Bruna & Mallat microcanonical synthesis): starting from init (random by default), minimize loss(scattering(st, x̂), target) with Adam. target may be a precomputed coefficient container or a field (its coefficients are taken first). The gradient is obtained through DifferentiationInterface, so backend is any ADTypes backend. The modulus discards local phase, so the result is a sample with matching statistics and the original is not recovered. Requires using DifferentiationInterface and an AD backend package.\n\nWith Enzyme, pass AutoEnzyme(; mode = Enzyme.set_runtime_activity(Enzyme.Reverse)): the cascade's closures capture constant filters alongside the active input, which Enzyme's static activity analysis cannot separate, and a plain AutoEnzyme() raises EnzymeRuntimeActivityError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.synthesize"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.scattering_loss","page":"API Reference","text":"scattering_loss(c, target) -> Real\n\nDefault synthesize objective: the normalized squared error between the coefficient container c and the target, summed over the first- and (when present) second-order coefficients, ‖S₁(c)−S₁(t)‖² + ‖S₂(c)−S₂(t)‖² divided by the target energy. Differentiable in c, so it composes with scattering(st, ·) under autodiff.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.scattering_loss"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Monogenic.MonogenicScatteringTransform","page":"API Reference","text":"MonogenicScatteringTransform{D}\n\nMonogenic scattering transform on a D-dimensional grid: an isotropic MonogenicFilterBank, the scattering path tree (strictly-increasing scale), a spectral plan, and the periodized MonogenicWorkspace the cascade runs in.\n\nD is the number of spatial axes in dims.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Monogenic.MonogenicScatteringTransform"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Monogenic.MonogenicFilterBank","page":"API Reference","text":"MonogenicFilterBank{D,T,A,W,R,MV}\n\nIsotropic band-pass wavelets wavelets (one per scale/sub-octave), the D scale-free Riesz multipliers riesz, and the Gaussian low-pass averaging. The wavelets and the low-pass are real functions of |k|; only the Riesz multipliers are complex.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Monogenic.MonogenicFilterBank"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Monogenic.ComputedMonogenicFilterBank","page":"API Reference","text":"ComputedMonogenicFilterBank{D,T,A,R,MV,PV}\n\nThe monogenic bank with its radial band-passes evaluated on demand into one scratch array, as in FilterBanks.ComputedFilterBank2D. The D Riesz multipliers are stored.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Monogenic.ComputedMonogenicFilterBank"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Monogenic.MonogenicWorkspace","page":"API Reference","text":"MonogenicWorkspace{T,D}\n\nThe periodized cascade plus what the monogenic amplitude needs on top of it: one real accumulator per resolution, and the Riesz-weighted band-passes R_d ψ_j periodized to each parent resolution.\n\nEach R_d ψ_j is stored as the periodization of the product, which differs from the product of the periodized factors. Level 1 stores none: the two full-resolution filters fuse in ScatteringCore.periodize_mul2!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Monogenic.MonogenicWorkspace"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Monogenic.build_monogenic_bank","page":"API Reference","text":"build_monogenic_bank([T=Float64,] dims::NTuple{D,Int}, J; Q=1) -> MonogenicFilterBank\n\nBuild a D-dimensional isotropic Morlet-style monogenic filter bank: J octaves × Q sub-octaves of radial band-pass wavelets whose radial profile is Filters.Morlet1D's (center frequency ξ_j = ξ₀·2^{-j/Q}, adjacent wavelets crossing at √½ of their peak), the Riesz multipliers, and Filters.gaussian_lowpass!. The band-pass and its Riesz components carry 2|ψ̂_j|² per frequency, so the wavelets are scaled by c = lp_scale_1d(Q)/2, which puts 2c² Σ_j g(|k|·2^{j/Q})² (g the radial profile) at a maximum of 1 over the bank continued over every scale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Monogenic.build_monogenic_bank"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Monogenic.riesz_multipliers","page":"API Reference","text":"riesz_multipliers(dims, ::Type{T}=Float64) -> NTuple{D, Array{Complex{T},D}}\n\nThe D Riesz-transform frequency multipliers R_d(k) = -i k_d/|k| over a grid of size dims, with R_d(0) = 0. On an even axis d the Nyquist bin holds k_d = ±½ at once, and there R_d is the mean of its two values, 0, so a real field's Riesz components are real. Scale-free, so one set serves every wavelet scale; Σ_d |R_d(k)|² = 1 off the DC bin and the Nyquist planes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Monogenic.riesz_multipliers"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Monogenic.monogenic_amplitude","page":"API Reference","text":"monogenic_amplitude(m0, riesz_components) -> A\n\nMonogenic amplitude A = √(m₀² + Σ_d m_d²) from the band-pass field m0 and the tuple/vector of Riesz component fields. Broadcast, so it is CPU/GPU/autodiff-generic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Monogenic.monogenic_amplitude"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Monogenic.monogenic_components","page":"API Reference","text":"monogenic_components(st, x, j) -> (; bandpass, riesz, amplitude, phase)\n\nThe monogenic decomposition of a real field x band-passed by isotropic wavelet j (1-based): the band-pass field bandpass = x ⋆ ψ_j, the D Riesz component fields riesz, the monogenic amplitude √(bandpass² + Σ|riesz|²), and the local monogenic phase = atan(‖riesz‖, bandpass). The Riesz vector's direction gives the local orientation (e.g. atan(riesz[2], riesz[1]) in 2D).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Monogenic.monogenic_components"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Monogenic.monogenic_scattering!","page":"API Reference","text":"monogenic_scattering!(coeffs, st, x) -> coeffs\n\nWrite the averaged monogenic coefficients of x into coeffs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Monogenic.monogenic_scattering!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.spherical_monogenic_scattering","page":"API Reference","text":"spherical_monogenic_scattering(pts_theta, pts_phi, lmax, J; max_order=2,\n                               transform=SpectralBackends.AutoSpectralBackend(), analysis=Plans.LeastSquares(),\n                               rtol=SphericalCore.default_rtol(transform), maxiter=500,\n                               weights=nothing, nufft=SpectralBackends.AutoSpectralBackend(),\n                               grid_lmax=2lmax)\n\nBuild a monogenic spherical scattering transform for a scalar field at scattered points (θ, φ) on S². The nonlinearity is the spherical monogenic amplitude A_j = √(U⁰_j² + |U^R_j|²), where U⁰_j is the difference-of-Gaussians band-pass and U^R_j is the spin-1 Riesz field R = ð∘(−Δ_S)^{-1/2}. The Riesz energy |U^R_j|² = |∇_S g_j|² (with g_j = (−Δ_S)^{-1/2} U⁰_j) is synthesised exactly from g_j's coefficients (SphericalCore.sphere_riesz_energy!). The cascade runs on the Gauss–Legendre grid and the keywords are as in spherical_scattering.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.spherical_monogenic_scattering"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.spherical_monogenic_components","page":"API Reference","text":"spherical_monogenic_components(st, field, j) -> (; bandpass, riesz, amplitude, phase, orientation)\n\nPointwise spherical monogenic decomposition of field band-passed at scale j — the S² analogue of the planar monogenic_components. Returns the band-pass field bandpass = U⁰_j, the spin-1 Riesz tangent vector riesz = (u_θ, u_φ) (U^R_j = ð∘(−Δ_S)^{-1/2} U⁰_j), the monogenic amplitude √(U⁰² + ‖U^R‖²), the phase = atan(‖U^R‖, U⁰), and the local orientation = atan(u_φ, u_θ) of the Riesz vector. st is a scattered or structured spherical monogenic transform. With the in-core direct SH plan (dependency-free) the Riesz field is synthesized as the surface gradient of g = (−Δ_S)^{-1/2}U⁰; with a NUFSHT-backed plan it uses spin-weighted synthesis — the two agree to solver accuracy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.spherical_monogenic_components"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.structured_spherical_monogenic_scattering","page":"API Reference","text":"structured_spherical_monogenic_scattering([T=Float64,] lmax, J; max_order=2,\n                                          transform=SpectralBackends.AutoSpectralBackend(),\n                                          rtol=1e-8, maxiter=500)\n\nStructured-grid counterpart of spherical_monogenic_scattering. transform names the SH transform as in structured_spherical_scattering (dependency-free direct SHT by default, fast exact SHT with using FastSphericalHarmonics).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.structured_spherical_monogenic_scattering"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringFields.scattering_field","page":"API Reference","text":"scattering_field(st, x; subsample) -> ScatteringField{1,2,3}D\n\nLocalized (Mallat) scattering transform: returns the per-path low-passed, subsampled fields. Methods are added by the per-dimension transform modules.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringFields.scattering_field"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringFields.scattering_field!","page":"API Reference","text":"scattering_field!(field, st, x) -> field\n\nIn-place localized scattering transform into a pre-allocated ScatteringField.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringFields.scattering_field!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringFields.ScatteringField1D","page":"API Reference","text":"ScatteringField1D{T,A,Tree,WS}\n\nLocalized 1D scattering field. data is (M, npaths); column p is the localized field of path p at the subsampled resolution M = N ÷ s. ws is the Cascade.FieldWorkspace that fills it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringFields.ScatteringField1D"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringFields.ScatteringField2D","page":"API Reference","text":"ScatteringField2D{T,A,Tree,WS}\n\nLocalized 2D scattering field. data is (My, Mx, npaths); slice [:, :, p] is the localized field of path p at subsampled resolution (My, Mx) = (Ny, Nx) ÷ s.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringFields.ScatteringField2D"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringFields.ScatteringField3D","page":"API Reference","text":"ScatteringField3D\n\nLocalized volumetric scattering with data shaped (Mz, My, Mx, npaths).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringFields.ScatteringField3D"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringFields.path_field","page":"API Reference","text":"path_field(sf, p) -> view\n\nView of path p's localized field, with the transform's spatial rank.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringFields.path_field"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringFields.subsample_factor","page":"API Reference","text":"subsample_factor(sf) -> Int\n\nThe decimation factor s applied to produce the field resolution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringFields.subsample_factor"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.ScatteringCoefficients1D","page":"API Reference","text":"ScatteringCoefficients1D{T,V,M,S0}\n\nImmutable container for 1D scattering coefficients. S0 is either a scalar, which update_S0 replaces by returning a new container, or a 1-element array, which it writes in place.\n\nType Parameters\n\nT: Element type\nV: 1D array type\nM: 2D array type  \nS0: S0 storage type (T for scalar, AbstractVector{T} for mutable)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.ScatteringCoefficients1D"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.ScatteringCoefficients2D","page":"API Reference","text":"ScatteringCoefficients2D{T,V,M,S0}\n\nImmutable container for scales × orientations scattering coefficients (2D, 3D and scattered planar). S0 is a scalar or a 1-element array, as in ScatteringCoefficients1D.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.ScatteringCoefficients2D"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.zeroth_order","page":"API Reference","text":"zeroth_order(c) -> Number\n\nThe zeroth-order coefficient S0, the field mean, which is complex for a complex field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.zeroth_order"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.first_order","page":"API Reference","text":"first_order(c) -> AbstractVector\n\nExtract the first-order (S1) scattering coefficients.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.first_order"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.second_order","page":"API Reference","text":"second_order(c) -> AbstractMatrix\n\nExtract the second-order (S2) scattering coefficients.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.second_order"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.flatten1d","page":"API Reference","text":"flatten1d(coeffs::ScatteringCoefficients1D) -> AbstractVector\n\nFlatten to [S0; S1; S2 upper triangle], keeping only the S2 entries with j2 > j1, in an array of element type flat_eltype.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.flatten1d"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.flatten2d","page":"API Reference","text":"flatten2d(coeffs::ScatteringCoefficients2D) -> AbstractVector\n\nFlatten to [S0; S1; S2 upper triangle], as flatten1d does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.flatten2d"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.flatten1d!","page":"API Reference","text":"flatten1d!(out, c) -> out\n\nZero-allocation flatten into a pre-allocated vector of length flatten_length(c).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.flatten1d!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.flatten2d!","page":"API Reference","text":"flatten2d!(out, c) -> out\n\nZero-allocation flatten into a pre-allocated vector of length flatten_length(c).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.flatten2d!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.flatten_length","page":"API Reference","text":"flatten_length(c) -> Int\n\nLength of the flattened coefficient vector [S0; S1; vec(S2 upper triangle)].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.flatten_length"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.flat_length","page":"API Reference","text":"flat_length(n) -> Int\nflat_row_s0() -> Int\nflat_row_s1(j, n) -> Int\nflat_row_s2(j1, j2, n) -> Int\n\nRow layout of the flattened coefficient vector [S0; S1; vec(S2 upper triangle)] for n wavelets, as produced by flatten1d!/flatten2d!. The S2 rows are present, zero, for coefficients that hold no S2.\n\nThe batched paths write flattened columns through these accessors, so they must match the order the flatten*! loops write.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.flat_length"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.flat_eltype","page":"API Reference","text":"flat_eltype(c) -> Type\n\nElement type a flattened column of c needs.\n\nS1 and S2 are moduli and stay real whatever the input is, but S0 is the field mean and is complex for a complex field, so the flat layout is as wide as the two together.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.flat_eltype"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.update_S0","page":"API Reference","text":"update_S0(c, val) -> c′\n\nThe coefficients c with S0 = val. A 1-element-array S0 is written in place and c returned; a scalar S0 gives a new container sharing c's S1 and S2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Coefficients.update_S0"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Scattering2D.compute_shape_sparsity","page":"API Reference","text":"compute_shape_sparsity(S1, S2, meta) -> (; sparsity, shape)\n\nReduced second-order descriptors (in the spirit of the reduced wavelet scattering transform, Allys et al. 2019; Cheng & Ménard 2021), as J × J matrices over scale pairs (j1, j2) with j2 > j1:\n\nsparsity (s₂₁): the orientation-averaged ratio ⟨S₂ / S₁⟩ — how much energy cascades from scale j1 to the coarser scale j2 (large for sparse/intermittent fields).\nshape (s₂₂): the anisotropy of the cascade — the normalized second angular harmonic ⟨S₂ · cos(2 Δθ)⟩ / ⟨S₂⟩ over orientation pairs, where Δθ = θ₂ − θ₁. It is ≈ 0 for statistically isotropic fields and departs from zero when the field has oriented structure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Scattering2D.compute_shape_sparsity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Reductions.normalized_coefficients","page":"API Reference","text":"normalized_coefficients(c) -> (; S0, s1, s2)\n\nAmplitude-normalized coefficients: s1[j] = S1[j]/S0 and s2[j1,j2] = S2[j1,j2]/S1[j1] (zero where S1[j1] == 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Reductions.normalized_coefficients"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Reductions.log_coefficients","page":"API Reference","text":"log_coefficients(c; pad=eps) -> (; S0, logS1, logS2)\n\nLog-coefficients log(S1 + pad), log(S2 + pad) (the small pad keeps structural zeros finite). Useful to gaussianize the heavy-tailed coefficients of intermittent fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Reductions.log_coefficients"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.scattering_batch","page":"API Reference","text":"scattering_batch(st::ScatteringCore.ScatteringTransform{1}, X; execution = SerialBackend()) -> Matrix\n\nApply a 1D scattering transform to a batch of signals X of size (N, B) (signals as columns), returning a (flatten_length, B) matrix whose column b is flatten1d(st(X[:, b])). The plan, the workspace and one coefficient container are reused across the batch. execution is where the batch runs, as in scattering_batch!.\n\n\n\n\n\nscattering_batch(st::ScatteringCore.ScatteringTransform{2}, X; execution = SerialBackend()) -> Matrix\n\nApply a 2D scattering transform to a batch of images X of size (Ny, Nx, B), returning a (flatten_length, B) matrix whose column b is flatten2d(st(X[:, :, b])). Plan and workspace are reused across the batch. execution is as in scattering_batch!.\n\n\n\n\n\nscattering_batch(st::ScatteringCore.ScatteringTransform{3}, X; execution = SerialBackend()) -> Matrix\n\nApply a 3D scattering transform to a batch of volumes X of size (Nz, Ny, Nx, B), returning a (flatten_length, B) matrix. Plan and workspace are reused across the batch. execution is as in scattering_batch!.\n\n\n\n\n\nscattering_batch(st::ScatteredPlanar.ScatteredPlanarScatteringTransform, X; execution = SerialBackend())\n    -> Matrix\n\nTransform every slice of X against one transform, returning a (flatten_length, B) matrix. X is (M, B), where M is the plan's point count. A transform built with ntrans = W > 1 runs the columns W at a time, through one execution per cascade step for each group, the last group padded with zero columns. execution is as in scattering_batch!.\n\n\n\n\n\nscattering_batch(st::SphericalCore.SphericalScatteringTransform, X; execution = SerialBackend()) -> Matrix\nscattering_batch(st::SphericalCore.SphericalMonogenicScatteringTransform, X; execution = SerialBackend())\n    -> Matrix\n\nTransform a stack of B fields sampled by the same spherical plan — X of size (field_size…, B), so (M, B) for a scattered point set and (nθ, nφ, B) on a structured grid. execution is as in scattering_batch!.\n\nRows follow Coefficients.flat_length. Scales are finest-first and second-order pairs satisfy j2 > j1, with row flat_row_s2(j1, j2, J).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.scattering_batch"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.scattering_batch!","page":"API Reference","text":"scattering_batch!(out, st, X; execution = SerialBackend(), workspace = nothing) -> out\n\nIn-place counterpart of scattering_batch: write the flattened coefficients of each slice of X into the preallocated (flatten_length, B) matrix out.\n\nexecution is where the batch runs: SerialBackend in this task, ThreadedBackend and GPUBackend through their extensions (OhMyThreads; KernelAbstractions, for the gridded transforms), and AutoBackend as Execution.resolve_backend resolves it. DistributedBackend and MPIBackend gather a new matrix, so they run through scattering_batch alone. A backend whose extension is not loaded throws.\n\nOn SerialBackend a gridded transform runs one slice at a time against its own single-slice plan, which on a CPU keeps each slice's working set in cache. Passing a batch_workspace runs the whole stack through one batched plan (Batched.batch_cascade!), as the GPU extension does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.scattering_batch!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.batch_coeffs","page":"API Reference","text":"batch_coeffs(st, T, S0T = T) -> coefficient container\n\nA coefficient container for st whose S0 is a 1-element array, so a loop over a batch writes every field in place.\n\nT is the element type of S1/S2, which are moduli; S0T is that of S0, the field mean, which is complex for a complex field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.batch_coeffs"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.flat_rows","page":"API Reference","text":"flat_rows(st) -> Int\n\nRows a flattened coefficient column of st occupies — the height of scattering_batch's output.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.flat_rows"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.batch_workspace","page":"API Reference","text":"batch_workspace(st, B; transform = nothing, fft_nthreads = 1, kwargs...) -> Batched.BatchWorkspace\n\nBuild the batched workspace for transform st at batch size B: a spectral plan over the whole (spatial…, B) stack plus the scratch Batched.batch_cascade! runs in, on the array type of st's own buffers. Build it once and pass it as workspace to scattering_batch! for every stack of B slices.\n\nThe stack's plan is of the kind st.plan is (Plans.plan_like), so a device-resident st gets a device workspace; a SpectralBackends tag as transform builds a host plan of that kind. fft_nthreads is the FFT library's own thread count, fixed in the plan; leave it at 1 when the batch or wavelet axis is already threaded. kwargs go to the plan builder.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.batch_workspace"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Batched.BatchWorkspace","page":"API Reference","text":"BatchWorkspace\n\nA spectral batch workspace with real-input staging and a full-resolution input spectrum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Batched.BatchWorkspace"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Batched.SpectralBatchWorkspace","page":"API Reference","text":"SpectralBatchWorkspace{T,D}\n\nPeriodized cascade buffers and per-field reductions for a stack of spectra.\n\nred has shape (1…, B); invN[l] normalizes the mean at resolution l.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Batched.SpectralBatchWorkspace"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Batched.batch_cascade!","page":"API Reference","text":"batch_cascade!(out, ws, X) -> out\n\nWrite the flattened scattering coefficients of every slice of X into the columns of out, which has Coefficients.flat_length(n) rows in the layout flatten1d!/flatten2d! produce. A complex X needs complex columns, for its complex S0.\n\nThe batched form of Cascade.cascade!, with each mean replaced by a per-slice reduction and each decimation tuple extended by a 1 for the stack axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Batched.batch_cascade!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.close_transform!","page":"API Reference","text":"close_transform!(st) -> nothing\n\nPlans.close_plan! every spectral plan st owns. st must not be in use by any task, and is not usable after.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.close_transform!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.decimation","page":"API Reference","text":"decimation(N, scale, α) -> NTuple{D,Int}\n\nPer-axis decimation for a wavelet of octave scale: 2^max(scale-α, 0), reduced on each axis to the largest power of two that divides N[d]. The clamp only reduces decimation, so any N is accepted at no cost in accuracy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.decimation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.PeriodizedWorkspace","page":"API Reference","text":"PeriodizedWorkspace{T,D}\n\nEverything the cascade runs in: one working array and one spectrum array per live decimation, the spectral plan at each of those sizes, and the filters periodized to each resolution they are applied at.\n\nEverything per-resolution is a vector indexed by level, with level[j] the level of wavelet j and level 1 the full grid. out[l] === work[l] when the plan inverts in place. spec[l] is empty except at levels that are a parent, where it holds Û₁ across that wavelet's children. D is the spatial rank of the resolutions; a batched workspace's arrays carry a trailing stack axis on top of it. means is a DeviceMeans on a device-resident workspace and nothing otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.PeriodizedWorkspace"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.DeviceMeans","page":"API Reference","text":"DeviceMeans{R,V,M,H}\n\nThe per-path sums of a single-field cascade on device arrays, accumulated on the device so that the cascade synchronises with the host once, after its last path: red is a (1, …, 1) reduction target, s1 holds one sum per wavelet and s2 one per (j1, j2) pair, and invlen[j] is one over the size of the grid wavelet j is produced on, which turns a sum into a mean.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.DeviceMeans"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.gather_means!","page":"API Reference","text":"gather_means!(S1, S2, means) -> nothing\n\nThe sums a cascade kept on the device, as means in the host arrays S1 and S2; nothing to do without means.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.gather_means!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.build","page":"API Reference","text":"build(fb, groups, dims, T, α, plan) -> PeriodizedWorkspace\n\ngroups is the cascade work list (j1, children, pathids); it determines which resolutions are live and which (wavelet, resolution) filter pairs are ever applied, so nothing unused is built. plan is the transform's full-grid plan and serves as level 1; the coarser levels are planned with its spectral backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.build"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.cascade!","page":"API Reference","text":"cascade!(S1, S2, ws, fb, groups, xfft) -> (S1, S2)\n\nBoth scattering orders in one grouped pass, each convolution produced directly on the grid its wavelet's band needs.\n\nOrder 1 multiplies by the full-resolution filter and periodizes the product. Order 2 multiplies Û₁ by ψ_{j₂} periodized to the parent's grid, then periodizes the product onto the child's; r₂ ≥ r₁, since admissibility makes the child strictly coarser.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.cascade!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.group!","page":"API Reference","text":"group!(S1, S2, ws, fb, g, xfft) -> nothing\n\nOne first-order wavelet and all of its children. Group j1 writes only S1[j1] and S2[j1, :], so tasks may run distinct groups concurrently, each with its own task_copy of the workspace. The caller clears S2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.group!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.scattering_values","page":"API Reference","text":"scattering_values(st, x) -> (S0, S1, S2)\n\nThe same periodized cascade as cascade!, written with allocating transforms and broadcasts so that reverse-mode backends can differentiate it. Dimension-generic; the per-dimension scattering methods wrap the result in their coefficient container.\n\nIt must compute the same function as st(x), decimation included: synthesize differentiates this to match coefficients read from st(x).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.scattering_values"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.task_copy","page":"API Reference","text":"task_copy(ws, fb) -> ws′\n\nA workspace usable concurrently with ws: fresh buffers and task-local plans, sharing the read-only periodized filters. Every task that runs group! needs its own.\n\nfb is the bank the copy runs against. For a computed bank it must be the task's own FilterBanks.task_bank, whose scratch the copy's level-1 filters alias.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.task_copy"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.with_plans","page":"API Reference","text":"with_plans(f, ws) -> ws′\n\nws with every plan replaced by f(plan), sharing all buffers and filters, for instrumenting or retargeting a cascade's transforms. f must preserve Plans.inplace_inverse, since the out/work aliasing follows it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.with_plans"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.FieldWorkspace","page":"API Reference","text":"FieldWorkspace{T,D}\n\nThe periodized cascade plus what the localized transform S_p = (|U_p| ⋆ φ_J) ↓ s needs on top of it: φ_J periodized to every level a modulus is produced at, and one buffer and plan at the output resolution.\n\nIts cascade is built with alias_out = false and decimates no wavelet past s (see field_oversampling), so every path is produced at or above the output resolution. At the default s = 2^(J-1) the cap changes nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.FieldWorkspace"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.build_field","page":"API Reference","text":"build_field(fb, groups, dims, T, α, s, φ; alloc, makeplan) -> FieldWorkspace\n\nWorkspace for the localized transform at output subsample factor s. φ is the full-resolution low-pass.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.build_field"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.field_workspace","page":"API Reference","text":"field_workspace(st, subsample) -> FieldWorkspace\n\nThe localized-field workspace for transform st at output factor subsample.\n\nst must have fields filter_bank, groups, dims, plan, buffer_input and pw. Buffers take buffer_input's array type and plans plan's kind, so a device-resident transform gets a device-resident workspace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.field_workspace"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.field_oversampling","page":"API Reference","text":"field_oversampling(fb, α, s) -> Int\n\nThe smallest oversampling at least α under which no wavelet decimates past s.\n\nr = 2^max(j-α, 0) ≤ s = 2^q iff α ≥ j - q, so raising α to maxscale - q caps every resolution at the output grid without changing the dyadic family any wavelet lands on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.field_oversampling"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.localize!","page":"API Reference","text":"localize!(dst, fw, spec, level) -> dst\n\ndst = real(ifft(spec ⋅ φ̂) ↓ (rout/r)), where spec is the spectrum of a modulus living at level. The product is periodized onto the output grid, so the inverse runs at output size.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.localize!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.field_group!","page":"API Reference","text":"field_group!(data, fw, fb, g, xfft, p1_first) -> nothing\n\nOne first-order wavelet and all of its children, written as localized fields into data's trailing path axis. The order-1 path id is p1_first + j1 - 1; the children's come from the group.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.field_group!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.field_cascade!","page":"API Reference","text":"field_cascade!(data, fw, fb, groups, xfft, root, p1_first) -> data\n\nEvery path's localized field. root is the order-0 path id, p1_first the first order-1 id.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Cascade.field_cascade!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.periodize_mul!","page":"API Reference","text":"periodize_mul!(dst, src, filt, r) -> dst\nperiodize!(dst, src, r) -> dst\n\ndst[k] = (1/∏r) Σ_m src[k + m∘n] * filt[k + m∘n], where n = size(dst) and m runs over the ∏r alias blocks: the spectrum of (src ⋆ filt) decimated by r per axis. For any spectrum Ŷ,\n\nifft_N(Ŷ)[1:r:end] == ifft_M(periodize_r(Ŷ)),   M = N ./ r\n\nwith the 1/∏r supplying the normalisation ifft_M lacks. Indices are 0-based on unshifted grids, so bin k of dst gathers bins k, k+n, k+2n, … of src; a singleton filt axis broadcasts. Both are broadcasts only, so they run on device arrays.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.periodize_mul!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.periodize_mul2!","page":"API Reference","text":"periodize_mul2!(dst, src, f1, f2, r) -> dst\n\nperiodize_mul! with two filters, dst[k] = (1/∏r) Σ_m src[·] * f1[·] * f2[·], in one pass: the monogenic band-pass R_d · ψ_j is a product of two full-resolution filters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.periodize_mul2!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore._modulus","page":"API Reference","text":"_modulus(z)\n\n|z| for the scattering nonlinearity, computed as sqrt(abs2(z)).\n\nIt agrees with abs(z) to one ulp while abs2(z) neither overflows nor underflows, i.e. for sqrt(floatmin) < |z| < sqrt(floatmax). A wavelet coefficient is O(‖x‖), so a field near those magnitudes must be rescaled before transforming.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore._modulus"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.periodize","page":"API Reference","text":"periodize(src, r) -> dst\n\nAllocating form of periodize! (see periodize_mul!), for the non-mutating forward that autodiff differentiates; it writes nothing in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.periodize"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.periodize_filter!","page":"API Reference","text":"periodize_filter!(dst, src, r) -> dst\n\nA filter periodized onto a coarser grid: dst[k] = Σ_m src[k + m∘n], with no 1/∏r, which periodize_mul! applies to the product being decimated.\n\nThe periodized filter keeps the original's frequencies. A Morlet evaluated afresh on the N/r grid is a different wavelet: its profile is a function of normalised angular frequency, so its peak lands at bin (N/r)·k0/2π, an octave lower per factor of two.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.periodize_filter!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Filters.gaussian_lowpass!","page":"API Reference","text":"gaussian_lowpass!(φ, σ) -> φ\ngaussian_lowpass(T, dims, J; sigma0=0.8) -> Array{T,D}\n\nIsotropic Gaussian low-pass φ̂(k) = exp(-|k|²σ²/2) on the FFT grid of dims, with k the angular frequency (2π·fftfreq per axis) and σ = sigma0·2^J a real-space width: the averaging filter φ_J of every filter bank, and of the localized transform S_p = (U_p ⋆ φ_J) ↓ s.\n\nφ̂(0) = 1, so averaging preserves a field's mean. Decimation by s keeps ⟨S_p⟩ = ⟨U_p⟩ when φ̂ vanishes on the lattice {2πm/s : m ≠ 0}; at s = 2^(J-1) its largest value there is exp(-(4π·sigma0)²/2), 1e-22 at sigma0 = 0.8.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Filters.gaussian_lowpass!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.plan_like","page":"API Reference","text":"plan_like(plan, prototype) -> plan′\nplan_like(plan, prototype, D; kwargs...) -> plan′\n\nA plan of the same kind as plan, sized for arrays like prototype, for a cascade that needs plans at resolutions chosen after the transform was built. The three-argument form transforms the leading D axes of prototype and leaves its trailing stack axis alone, with kwargs going to the plan builder. A device plan has no spectral-backend tag, so its method plans on prototype's array type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.plan_like"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.inplace_inverse","page":"API Reference","text":"inplace_inverse(plan) -> Bool\n\nWhether inverse_transform!(x, plan, x) is valid, letting a transform alias its convolution output with its multiply scratch. Default false: the direct-sum plan ping-pongs through its own scratch, and over an odd number of axes its first pass writes into its input.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.inplace_inverse"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.FilterBank1D","page":"API Reference","text":"FilterBank1D{T,V,W,MV}\n\nComplete 1D filter bank for the scattering transform. V is the per-filter array type, W the wavelet collection and MV the metadata collection.\n\nFields\n\nwavelets::W: wavelet filters in the Fourier domain (W<:AbstractVector{V})\naveraging::V: low-pass averaging (scaling) filter\nmeta::MV: per-wavelet WaveletMeta\nJ::Int: number of octaves (scales)\nQ::Int: wavelets per octave\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.FilterBank1D"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.FilterBank2D","page":"API Reference","text":"FilterBank2D{T,M,W,MV}\n\nComplete 2D filter bank with oriented wavelets. M is the per-filter matrix type, W the wavelet collection and MV the metadata collection.\n\nFields\n\nwavelets::W: oriented wavelet filters (W<:AbstractVector{M})\naveraging::M: low-pass averaging filter\nmeta::MV: per-wavelet WaveletMeta\nJ::Int: number of scales\nL::Int: number of orientations\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.FilterBank2D"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.FilterBank3D","page":"API Reference","text":"FilterBank3D{T,A,W,MV}\n\nComplete 3D oriented Morlet filter bank: J scales × n_orient sphere directions, plus a low-pass averaging filter. A is the per-filter array type, W the wavelet collection and MV the metadata collection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.FilterBank3D"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.WaveletMeta","page":"API Reference","text":"WaveletMeta{T}\n\nPer-wavelet metadata.\n\nFields\n\nscale::Int: octave index j\nq::Int: sub-octave index within the octave (1D, 0..Q-1); 0 otherwise\norient::Int: orientation index (0..L-1 in 2D, 0..n_orient-1 in 3D); 0 in 1D\nj_eff::T: effective log-scale that orders paths, j + q/Q in 1D and T(j) in 2D and 3D. A second-order path is admissible iff j_eff(child) > j_eff(parent), which in 2D and 3D admits every orientation pair at a strictly coarser scale.\ncenter_freq::T: wavelet center frequency\ntheta::T: orientation angle in radians (2D); 0 otherwise\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.WaveletMeta"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.build_filter_bank1d","page":"API Reference","text":"build_filter_bank1d([T = Float64,] N, J; Q = 1) -> FilterBank1D\n\nBuild a 1D Morlet filter bank with dyadic scales, Littlewood–Paley normalized by lp_scale_1d.\n\nArguments\n\nN::Int: Signal length (FFT size)\nJ::Int: Maximum scale (number of octaves)\nQ::Int: Wavelets per octave (default 1 for dyadic, 8 for high Q)\n\nReturns\n\nFilterBank1D: Complete filter bank with J scales\n\n\n\n\n\nbuild_filter_bank1d(T, N, J, filters::AbstractFilterStorage; Q = 1)\nbuild_filter_bank3d(T, N, J, filters::AbstractFilterStorage; n_orient = 6)\n\nComputedFilters() returns a computed bank, which evaluates each wavelet on demand; StoredFilters() returns the stored bank.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.build_filter_bank1d"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.build_filter_bank2d","page":"API Reference","text":"build_filter_bank2d([T = Float64,] N, J; L = 8) -> FilterBank2D\n\nBuild a 2D oriented Morlet filter bank, Littlewood–Paley normalized by lp_scale_2d.\n\nArguments\n\nN::NTuple{2,Int}: Image dimensions (Ny, Nx)\nJ::Int: Number of dyadic scales\nL::Int: Number of orientations (default 8, evenly spaced)\n\nReturns\n\nFilterBank2D: Complete 2D filter bank\n\n\n\n\n\nbuild_filter_bank2d(T, N, J, filters::AbstractFilterStorage; L = 8)\n\nComputedFilters() returns a ComputedFilterBank2D, which evaluates each wavelet on demand; StoredFilters() returns the stored bank.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.build_filter_bank2d"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.build_filter_bank3d","page":"API Reference","text":"build_filter_bank3d([T = Float64,] N, J; n_orient = 6) -> FilterBank3D\n\nBuild a 3D oriented Morlet filter bank with J dyadic scales and n_orient near-uniform orientations on the sphere (Fibonacci spiral), Littlewood–Paley normalized by lp_scale_3d.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.build_filter_bank3d"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.ComputedFilterBank1D","page":"API Reference","text":"ComputedFilterBank1D{T,V,MV,WV}\nComputedFilterBank3D{T,A,MV,WV}\n\nThe 1D and 3D counterparts of ComputedFilterBank2D.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.ComputedFilterBank1D"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.ComputedFilterBank2D","page":"API Reference","text":"ComputedFilterBank2D{T,M,MV,WV}\n\nA 2D bank that stores its wavelets' parameters and evaluates one, scaled by the Littlewood–Paley constant, into a single scratch array on each filter_at call.\n\nIt holds two grid-sized arrays, the low-pass and the scratch, against J·L+1 for a stored bank, and costs two exp per grid point per application. The scratch is mutable, so tasks cannot share one bank; task_bank gives each its own scratch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.ComputedFilterBank2D"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.AbstractFilterStorage","page":"API Reference","text":"AbstractFilterStorage\n\nHow a transform holds its wavelets, the filters keyword of its constructor: StoredFilters or ComputedFilters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.AbstractFilterStorage"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.StoredFilters","page":"API Reference","text":"StoredFilters() <: AbstractFilterStorage\n\nEvery wavelet stored in the Fourier domain on the transform's grid, the default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.StoredFilters"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.ComputedFilters","page":"API Reference","text":"ComputedFilters() <: AbstractFilterStorage\n\nEach wavelet's parameters stored, and the wavelet evaluated on demand into one scratch array (see ComputedFilterBank2D), for grids whose stored bank does not fit in memory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.ComputedFilters"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.filter_storage","page":"API Reference","text":"filter_storage(fb) -> AbstractFilterStorage\n\nThe filters that builds a bank like fb: StoredFilters() or ComputedFilters().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.filter_storage"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.nwavelets","page":"API Reference","text":"nwavelets(fb) -> Int\n\nHow many wavelets the bank holds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.nwavelets"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.filter_at","page":"API Reference","text":"filter_at(fb, j) -> AbstractArray\n\nThe bank's j-th wavelet in the Fourier domain. For a computed bank this is its shared scratch, valid until the next call.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.filter_at"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.iscomputed","page":"API Reference","text":"iscomputed(fb) -> Bool\n\nWhether filter_at returns the bank's shared scratch. A caller that then needs two filters live at once, or a filter at a resolution the bank does not hold, must copy it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.iscomputed"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.task_bank","page":"API Reference","text":"task_bank(fb) -> fb′\n\nA bank usable concurrently with fb. Stored banks are read-only and are returned as they are; a computed bank gets its own scratch, sharing everything else.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.task_bank"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.batch_views","page":"API Reference","text":"batch_views(fb, spatial) -> Vector\n\nThe bank's filters reshaped to (spatial…, 1), so they broadcast over a batch axis.\n\nFor a computed bank every entry is the same view of its scratch, so a caller must call filter_at(fb, j) immediately before using entry j.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.batch_views"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.littlewood_paley","page":"API Reference","text":"littlewood_paley(fb) -> A\n\nThe bank's Littlewood–Paley sum for a real field, A(ω) = |φ̂(ω)|² + ½ Σ_λ (|ψ̂_λ(ω)|² + |ψ̂_λ(−ω)|²), on its frequency grid. The wavelet transform of a real field is bounded by minimum(A)·‖x‖² ≤ ‖Wx‖² ≤ maximum(A)·‖x‖², and Inverse.iwavelet! divides by A.\n\n\n\n\n\nlittlewood_paley(fb::AnyMonogenicBank) -> A\n\nA(k) = |φ̂(k)|² + Σ_j |ψ̂_j(k)|² (1 + Σ_d |R_d(k)|²), the energy the band-pass, Riesz and low-pass outputs of a real field carry per frequency.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.littlewood_paley"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.lp_scale_1d","page":"API Reference","text":"lp_scale_1d(Q; r=√½) -> c\n\nThe wavelet scale of a 1D Morlet bank with Q wavelets per octave: ½ Σ_j |c·ψ̂_j(ω)|² peaks at 1 over the bank continued over every scale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.lp_scale_1d"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.lp_scale_2d","page":"API Reference","text":"lp_scale_2d(L; sigma0=0.8) -> c\n\nThe wavelet scale of a 2D Morlet bank with L orientations: ½ Σ_{j,ℓ} (|c·ψ̂_{j,ℓ}(k)|² + |c·ψ̂_{j,ℓ}(−k)|²) peaks at 1 over the bank continued over every scale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.lp_scale_2d"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.lp_scale_3d","page":"API Reference","text":"lp_scale_3d(n_orient; sigma0=0.8) -> c\n\nThe wavelet scale of a 3D Morlet bank over n_orient Fibonacci directions: ½ Σ_{j,o} (|c·ψ̂_{j,o}(k)|² + |c·ψ̂_{j,o}(−k)|²) peaks at 1 over the bank continued over every scale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.lp_scale_3d"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.negated","page":"API Reference","text":"negated(A) -> B\n\nB[k] = A[−k] on the DFT grid, with each index taken modulo its axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.FilterBanks.negated"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Filters.Morlet1D","page":"API Reference","text":"Morlet1D{T<:Real}\n\n1D Morlet wavelet in frequency domain, over normalized frequency ω ∈ [0, ½]:\n\nΨ(ω) = exp(-(ω-ξ)²/2σ²) - κ exp(-ω²/2σ²),   κ = exp(-(ξ/σ)²/2)\n\nσ is a frequency-domain width. κ sets Ψ(0) = 0, so the wavelet has zero mean. The filter is analytic: zero for ω < 0.\n\nType Parameters\n\nT: Element type (Float32, Float64, etc.)\n\nFields\n\ncenter_freq::T: Center frequency ξ\nbandwidth::T: Frequency-domain width σ\nN::Int: Filter length (FFT size)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Filters.Morlet1D"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Filters.Morlet2D","page":"API Reference","text":"Morlet2D{T<:Real}\n\n2D oriented Morlet wavelet in the frequency domain, k the angular wavenumber:\n\nΨ(k) = exp(-((k∥ - k₀)²σ∥² + k⊥²σ⊥²)/2) - β exp(-(k∥²σ∥² + k⊥²σ⊥²)/2),   β = exp(-(k₀σ∥)²/2)\n\nfor k∥ ≥ 0 and zero otherwise, with k∥ = kx cos θ + ky sin θ, k⊥ = -kx sin θ + ky cos θ. The widths are real-space: σ∥ = sigma0·2ʲ, σ⊥ = σ∥·L/4, and k₀ = 3π/(4·2ʲ). The envelope's angular standard deviation at |k| = k₀ is then 4/(L·k₀σ∥), which at the default sigma0 is 0.68·π/L: a fixed fraction of the spacing π/L of the L orientations.\n\nFields\n\ncenter_freq::T: k₀\nsigma_par::T, sigma_perp::T: real-space widths along and across the orientation\ntheta::T: orientation angle in radians, from the second array axis\nbeta::T: zero-mean correction\nN::NTuple{2,Int}: filter dimensions (Ny, Nx)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Filters.Morlet2D"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Filters.Morlet3D","page":"API Reference","text":"Morlet3D{T<:Real}\n\n3D oriented Morlet wavelet in the frequency domain, a bump centered at k₀ n̂ for a unit direction n̂, analytic on the half-space k·n̂ ≥ 0: Morlet2D's envelope with k∥ = k·n̂ and k⊥ = |k − k∥ n̂|. The real-space widths are σ∥ = sigma0·2ʲ and σ⊥ = σ∥·√(π·n_orient)/8, the 2D rule σ⊥ = σ∥·π/(4Δθ) at the spacing Δθ = √(4π/n_orient) of n_orient directions spread over the sphere.\n\nFields\n\ncenter_freq::T: |k₀|\nsigma_par::T, sigma_perp::T: real-space envelope widths along / perpendicular to n̂\ndirection::NTuple{3,T}: unit orientation n̂\nbeta::T: zero-mean correction\nN::NTuple{3,Int}: grid dimensions\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Filters.Morlet3D"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Filters.frequency_response","page":"API Reference","text":"frequency_response(m::Morlet1D{T}) -> Vector{T}\n\nThe frequency response Ψ(ω) of m on its N-point FFT grid, zero at negative frequencies.\n\n\n\n\n\nfrequency_response(m::Morlet2D{T}) -> Matrix{T}\n\nThe 2D frequency response Ψ(kx, ky) of an oriented Morlet wavelet, on its FFT grid.\n\n\n\n\n\nfrequency_response(m::Morlet3D{T}) -> Array{T,3}\n\n3D frequency response Ψ(kx,ky,kz) of an oriented Morlet wavelet.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Filters.frequency_response"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Filters.frequency_response!","page":"API Reference","text":"frequency_response!(Ψ, m) -> Ψ\n\nWrite the response into Ψ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Filters.frequency_response!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Filters._alias_rms","page":"API Reference","text":"_alias_rms(f, n, I) -> value\n\nf at the normalized frequencies of bin I of an n-point grid, f taking an NTuple of them. On an even axis's Nyquist bin −½ and +½ are one bin, so there the value is the root mean square of f over every sign of those coordinates. A real field's Littlewood–Paley sum at that bin is then the mean of the continuous sum over its aliases: an analytic wavelet centred at +½ keeps the bin.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Filters._alias_rms"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Filters.fibonacci_directions","page":"API Reference","text":"fibonacci_directions(n, ::Type{T}=Float64) -> Vector{NTuple{3,T}}\n\nn near-uniform unit directions on the sphere (Fibonacci spiral), used as 3D wavelet orientations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Filters.fibonacci_directions"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.PathGraph.ScatteringTree","page":"API Reference","text":"ScatteringTree{IV,RV}\n\nFlat CSR description of the scattering tree: wavelet indices into the filter bank and per-path order labels. The containers are parametric; build_tree produces Vector{Int} and Vector{UnitRange{Int}}.\n\nFields\n\npath_data: concatenated wavelet-index lists for all paths\npath_ptr: CSR offsets (length npaths+1); path p is path_data[path_ptr[p]:path_ptr[p+1]-1]\norder: scattering order of each path (0, 1, 2, …)\nby_order: by_order[o+1] is the contiguous range of path ids of order o\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.PathGraph.ScatteringTree"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.PathGraph.build_tree","page":"API Reference","text":"build_tree(j_eff, max_order) -> ScatteringTree\n\nEnumerate all admissible paths up to max_order from the per-wavelet effective log-scales j_eff (typically [m.j_eff for m in filter_bank.meta]). Admissibility: j_eff strictly increasing along the path. Paths are laid out grouped by order (order 0, then 1, then 2, …), so by_order ranges are contiguous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.PathGraph.build_tree"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.PathGraph.order2_groups","page":"API Reference","text":"order2_groups(tree, nw) -> Vector{Tuple{Int,Vector{Int},Vector{Int}}}\n\nEvery first-order wavelet j1 with its admissible order-2 children j2 and the tree path ids of those (j1, j2) pairs, longest group first.\n\nThe cascade computes each first-order modulus once per group, and the localized-field output is indexed by the path ids. Longest-first order balances the parallel backends, since the child count varies across scales.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.PathGraph.order2_groups"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.PathGraph.order_range","page":"API Reference","text":"order_range(tree, o) -> UnitRange\n\nContiguous range of path ids with scattering order o.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.PathGraph.order_range"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.PathGraph.path_indices","page":"API Reference","text":"path_indices(tree, p) -> view\n\nWavelet indices of path p (empty for the order-0 root), as a non-allocating view.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.PathGraph.path_indices"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.PathGraph.npaths","page":"API Reference","text":"npaths(tree) -> Int\n\nTotal number of paths (including the order-0 root).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.PathGraph.npaths"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.AbstractScatteringPlan","page":"API Reference","text":"AbstractScatteringPlan\n\nSupertype for spectral transform plans. A plan implements forward_transform! and inverse_transform!; the in-core default is DirectSumPlan, with fast paths (FFTW, AbstractFFTs/device, FINUFFT, NonuniformFFTs) provided by extensions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.AbstractScatteringPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.DirectSumPlan","page":"API Reference","text":"DirectSumPlan{T,V,D,S}\n\nDirect-summation DFT plan: evaluates X_k = Σ_n x_n e^{-2πi kn/N} (and its inverse) by direct summation, separably over the leading D dimensions, leaving a trailing batch axis of length nbatch untouched. Memory is O(N): a per-axis table of roots of unity and its conjugate. scratch is nothing for D == 1 and a full-size complex array otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.DirectSumPlan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.forward_transform!","page":"API Reference","text":"forward_transform!(out, plan, x) -> out\n\nIn-place forward (fft-convention) spectral transform. Methods provided by concrete plans.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.forward_transform!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.inverse_transform!","page":"API Reference","text":"inverse_transform!(out, plan, x) -> out\n\nIn-place inverse (ifft-convention, 1/N-scaled) spectral transform.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.inverse_transform!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.forward_transform","page":"API Reference","text":"forward_transform(plan, x) -> X̂\ninverse_transform(plan, x) -> x\n\nNon-mutating counterparts of forward_transform! and inverse_transform!, for the differentiable scattering(st, x). They allocate their output and mutate neither the plan's scratch nor x. DirectSumPlan contracts each axis against its twiddle table in scalar loops, so it accepts any element type, ForwardDiff.Dual included, and every AD backend traces it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.forward_transform"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.inverse_transform","page":"API Reference","text":"inverse_transform(plan, x) -> x\n\nSee forward_transform.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.inverse_transform"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.make_plan","page":"API Reference","text":"make_plan(spectral, T, dims; batch = nothing, kwargs...) -> AbstractScatteringPlan\n\nBuild the spectral plan selected by spectral for arrays whose leading dimensions are dims and whose element type is Complex{T}: arrays of size dims for batch = nothing, and (dims…, batch) stacks for an integer batch.\n\nSpectralBackends.DirectSumSpectralBackend is the dependency-free in-core default; SpectralBackends.FFTSpectralBackend requires using FFTW; SpectralBackends.AutoSpectralBackend takes the FFTW fast path when its extension is loaded and otherwise the in-core direct sum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.make_plan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.make_scattered_plan","page":"API Reference","text":"make_scattered_plan(spectral, x, y, ms, T; period, origin = (0, 0), analysis, maxiter, rtol, damp,\n                    tol, ntrans, nthreads) -> AbstractScatteringPlan\n\nBuild the scattered/nonuniform planar plan selected by spectral over points (x, y) and a uniform mode grid of size ms. SpectralBackends.DirectSumSpectralBackend is the dependency-free exact NUDFT; FlowTransformBindings.FINUFFTBackend() and FlowTransformBindings.NonuniformFFTsBackend() select a nonuniform-FFT library; SpectralBackends.AutoSpectralBackend takes NonuniformFFTs when it is loaded and FINUFFT when only it is, and the exact direct sum when neither is.\n\nperiod is the domain's length along each axis and origin its lower corner, each a number for both axes or a 2-tuple. A point lies at 2π·mod(x - origin, period)/period on the plan's 2π-periodic domain, so mode k has wavelength period/k whatever the point set.\n\nntrans is the batch width and nthreads the library's own thread count (0, the default, takes Threads.nthreads()). The exact direct sum has no tolerance and runs on one thread, so it accepts tol and nthreads and ignores them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.make_scattered_plan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.available","page":"API Reference","text":"available(spectral) -> Bool\n\nWhether the extension implementing spectral is loaded: true from that extension, so the transform AutoSpectralBackend takes follows from the loaded methods.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.available"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.spectral_backend","page":"API Reference","text":"spectral_backend(plan) -> SpectralBackends.AbstractSpectralBackend\n\nThe spectral-backend tag that rebuilds plan on another process. A plan that a tag cannot rebuild, such as a device-resident FFT plan, throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.spectral_backend"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.task_local_plan","page":"API Reference","text":"task_local_plan(plan) -> plan\n\nA plan equivalent to plan that is safe to use concurrently with it. Stateless plans (FFTW, AbstractFFTs) return themselves; plans carrying mutable scratch return a copy that shares their read-only tables and owns fresh scratch. Called once per task by the parallel backends.\n\nA method that builds a new library plan takes its thread count from per_task_nthreads, and the caller must close_plan! that plan when the task ends.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.task_local_plan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.batch_width","page":"API Reference","text":"batch_width(plan) -> Int\n\nNumber of co-located fields plan transforms per execution: 1 unless it was built for a batch. A nonuniform plan's width is fixed at construction, so it transforms exactly that many fields per execution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.batch_width"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.plan_points","page":"API Reference","text":"plan_points(plan) -> (x, y)\n\nThe scattered sample locations a nonuniform plan was built on, already mapped onto its 2π-periodic domain, from which a transform is rebuilt on another process. Rebuilding from these requires period = (2π, 2π), since they are already scaled.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.plan_points"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.plan_analysis","page":"API Reference","text":"plan_analysis(plan) -> (; analysis, maxiter, rtol, damp, tol, nthreads)\n\nHow a scattered-planar plan turns samples into modes, as the keyword arguments its constructor takes.\n\nA plan rebuilt on the same points needs these too: on irregular sampling the type-1 adjoint and the least-squares inversion are different transforms. tol is nothing for the exact direct sum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.plan_analysis"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.DirectNUFFTPlan","page":"API Reference","text":"DirectNUFFTPlan{T,TB,CV,RV,WS}\n\nExact direct-summation NUDFT between M scattered points and an ms mode grid, transforming batch_width(plan) co-located fields per call. Memory is O(M·(ms₁ + ms₂)): one exponential table per axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.DirectNUFFTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.NUFFTScatteringPlan","page":"API Reference","text":"NUFFTScatteringPlan\n\nScattered-planar plan over FlowTransformBindings NUFFT plans on fixed points: a complex plan for synthesis and for complex samples, and a real plan for real samples and the real solve. B is the number of co-located fields each execution transforms, and transform the NUFFT library tag the plans run on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.NUFFTScatteringPlan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.nufft_scattered_plan","page":"API Reference","text":"nufft_scattered_plan(backend, x, y, ms, T; period, origin = (0, 0), analysis, maxiter, rtol, damp,\n                     tol, ntrans, nthreads) -> NUFFTScatteringPlan\n\nThe scattered-planar plan over points (x, y) and mode grid ms on a FlowTransformBindings NUFFT backend, on a domain of length period with lower corner origin (see make_scattered_plan). nthreads = 0 takes the session's Threads.nthreads().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.nufft_scattered_plan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.with_fft_nthreads","page":"API Reference","text":"with_fft_nthreads(f, n) -> f()\n\nRun f, which builds plans, under FFTW.jl's planner lock with FFTW's planner thread count set to n and restored after (FFTW.set_num_threads(f, n)); without FFTW loaded, just call f().\n\nFFTW's planner state is process-global and shared by every library that plans on libfftw3, and a plan keeps the thread count it was built with, so every plan builder here sets the count through this. The fallback takes args... so the FFTW extension's two-argument method is more specific and adds a method; redefining this one is an error during precompilation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.with_fft_nthreads"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.per_task_nthreads","page":"API Reference","text":"per_task_nthreads(requested) -> Int\n\nThread count for a library plan that task_local_plan builds inside a task: requested when positive, else 1, since the tasks already occupy the cores.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.per_task_nthreads"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.close_plan!","page":"API Reference","text":"close_plan!(plan) -> nothing\n\nRelease the foreign-library resources plan owns without waiting for garbage collection. A no-op by default, and safe to call more than once. Whoever builds a plan for a task closes it when the task ends; cuFINUFFT plans are released only by this.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.close_plan!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.wavelet_convolve","page":"API Reference","text":"wavelet_convolve(signal_fft, filter_fft, plan) -> AbstractArray\n\nThe inverse transform of signal_fft .* filter_fft through plan, in a new array; wavelet_convolve! writes in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.wavelet_convolve"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.wavelet_convolve!","page":"API Reference","text":"wavelet_convolve!(out, signal_fft, filter_fft, plan, buffer)\n\nWavelet convolution without allocation: multiplies signal_fft .* filter_fft into buffer, then applies the inverse spectral transform via Plans.inverse_transform!(out, plan, buffer).\n\nout and buffer must both be pre-allocated complex arrays of the same size.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.wavelet_convolve!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.apply_modulus","page":"API Reference","text":"apply_modulus(signal) -> AbstractArray\n\n|signal| elementwise, in a new real array; apply_modulus! writes in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.apply_modulus"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.apply_modulus!","page":"API Reference","text":"apply_modulus!(out, signal)\n\nIn-place modulus. Stores |signal| in pre-allocated out. Zero allocation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.apply_modulus!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.modulus_mean","page":"API Reference","text":"modulus_mean(signal) -> Real\n\n⟨|signal|⟩ in one reduction, writing no modulus field: the cascade's leaf case, where no coarser scale reads it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.modulus_mean"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.modulus_mean!","page":"API Reference","text":"modulus_mean!(out, signal) -> Real\n\nWrite |signal| into out, real or complex, and return ⟨|signal|⟩: the case where a coarser scale reads the modulus field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.modulus_mean!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.spatial_average","page":"API Reference","text":"spatial_average(signal) -> Number\n\nThe global mean of signal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.spatial_average"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.task_workspace","page":"API Reference","text":"task_workspace(st) -> st′\n\nA transform equivalent to st that shares its read-only parts — filter bank, path tree, work list — but owns fresh buffers and a task-local spectral plan, so the two can run concurrently. Methods are defined per transform type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteringCore.task_workspace"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.AbstractAnalysis","page":"API Reference","text":"AbstractAnalysis\n\nPolicy for recovering spectral coefficients from scattered samples.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.AbstractAnalysis"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.WeightedAdjoint","page":"API Reference","text":"Analyze samples by a quadrature-weighted adjoint transform.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.WeightedAdjoint"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.LeastSquares","page":"API Reference","text":"Analyze samples by a least-squares fit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.LeastSquares"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.default_solver_rtol","page":"API Reference","text":"default_solver_rtol(::Type{T}, spectral, tol) -> T\n\nStopping tolerance for the scattered least-squares solve, set by the accuracy of the transform under it.\n\nThe direct sum evaluates an exact adjoint pair, so the target is sqrt(Base.eps(T)). A fast NUFFT's type-1 and type-2 are adjoints only to its tolerance tol (the library default for T when nothing), so the target is max(10·tol, sqrt(Base.eps(T))).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.default_solver_rtol"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.warn_underdetermined","page":"API Reference","text":"warn_underdetermined(M, ms, analysis, damp) -> nothing\n\nWarn, once, that a solve has more modes than samples and no damping.\n\nWith prod(ms) > M at least prod(ms) - M mode directions are constrained by no sample, and LSMR returns the minimum-norm iterate. Whether that is the answer wanted, or whether to reduce ms, add samples, or set damp for Tikhonov regularisation, is the caller's choice; no λ is chosen for them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.warn_underdetermined"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.fftw_plan","page":"API Reference","text":"fftw_plan(T, dims; batch, planning, fft_nthreads) -> AbstractScatteringPlan\n\nBuild the FFTW-backed plan, a method the FFTW extension defines; this fallback throws an ArgumentError naming using FFTW.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.fftw_plan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Plans.abstractffts_plan","page":"API Reference","text":"abstractffts_plan(dummy_device_array; region = 1:ndims(dummy_device_array), fft_nthreads = 1)\n\nFFT plan for the array type of dummy_device_array, built through AbstractFFTs.plan_fft and plan_ifft, so a CuArray gives a cuFFT plan and a ROCArray a rocFFT plan. region selects the transformed dimensions, e.g. (1, 2) for a (Ny, Nx, B) stack. The KernelAbstractions extension defines the method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Plans.abstractffts_plan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Execution.resolve_backend","page":"API Reference","text":"resolve_backend(backend) -> AbstractLocalBackend\n\nConcrete backends pass through unchanged — a request is honoured exactly or refused, never silently downgraded. AutoBackend resolves on real capability: ThreadedBackend when there is more than one thread and the OhMyThreads extension is loaded, otherwise SerialBackend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Execution.resolve_backend"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Execution.check_available","page":"API Reference","text":"check_available(backend) -> backend\n\nReturn backend if its extension is loaded, and otherwise throw an ArgumentError naming the package to load. An AutoBackend must go through resolve_backend first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Execution.check_available"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Execution.available","page":"API Reference","text":"available(execution) -> Bool\n\nWhether the extension for execution is loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Execution.available"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Scattering1D.cascade!","page":"API Reference","text":"cascade!(S1, S2, st, signal_fft) -> (S1, S2)\n\nBoth scattering orders in one pass over the tree, grouped by first-order wavelet:\n\nfor (j1, children):  U₁ = |x ⋆ ψ_j1| ;  S1[j1] = ⟨U₁⟩\n                     Û₁ = fft(U₁)     ;  S2[j1,j2] = ⟨|U₁ ⋆ ψ_j2|⟩  for each child\n\nOne U₁/Û₁ pair is live at a time, and a wavelet with no admissible child reduces to a single ⟨|·|⟩. signal_fft is read only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Scattering1D.cascade!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Scattering2D.cascade!","page":"API Reference","text":"cascade!(S1, S2, st, image_fft) -> (S1, S2)\n\nBoth scattering orders in one grouped pass, as in the 1D cascade!. An order-2 path pairs any two orientations at strictly increasing scale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Scattering2D.cascade!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.Scattering3D.cascade!","page":"API Reference","text":"cascade!(S1, S2, st, vol_fft) -> (S1, S2)\n\nBoth scattering orders in one grouped pass, as in the 1D cascade!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.Scattering3D.cascade!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteredPlanar.ScatteredPlanarScatteringTransform","page":"API Reference","text":"ScatteredPlanarScatteringTransform\n\nScattering transform of a field sampled at scattered planar points, as built by scattered_planar_scattering: the samples analysed onto the ms mode grid by the spectral plan, then the cascade of the gridded Scattering2D transform on that grid, with its oriented Morlet bank.\n\nA transform built with ntrans = B > 1 analyses B fields per call: its sample and mode buffers are (M, B) and (ms…, B), including a trailing batch axis at width 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteredPlanar.ScatteredPlanarScatteringTransform"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteredPlanar.scattered_planar_scattering!","page":"API Reference","text":"scattered_planar_scattering!(coeffs, st, x) -> coeffs\n\nIn-place counterpart of the callable: write into a preallocated ScatteringCoefficients2D. Allocation-free when coeffs carries a 1-element S0, which Coefficients.update_S0 writes in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteredPlanar.scattered_planar_scattering!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.ScatteredPlanar.scattered_planar_scattering_batch!","page":"API Reference","text":"scattered_planar_scattering_batch!(S0, S1, S2, st, X) -> (; S0, S1, S2)\n\nCascade over a stack of B fields sampled at the same points: X is (M, B), S0 is (B), S1 is (nw, B) and S2 is (nw, nw, B), (0, 0, B) without a second order.\n\nThe analysis covers the whole stack in one execution, and each field's cascade then runs on the mode grid; st must have been built with ntrans = B.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.ScatteredPlanar.scattered_planar_scattering_batch!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.AbstractSphericalPlan","page":"API Reference","text":"AbstractSphericalPlan\n\nSupertype for spherical spectral plans (scattered NUFSHT, structured SHT, …). A concrete plan must implement sphere_coeffs, sphere_apply!, and sphere_mean.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.AbstractSphericalPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.DirectSHTSphericalPlan","page":"API Reference","text":"DirectSHTSphericalPlan\n\nThe in-core scattered-sphere plan over M points at band limit lmax, transforming B co-located fields per call. Analysis is the least-squares fit of the real spherical harmonics of degree ≤ lmax by LSMR (FlowTransformBindings.lsmr!), weighted by the quadrature weights w, which sum to 1: min Σⱼ wⱼ (fⱼ − Σ a_ℓm Y_ℓm(xⱼ))². With analysis = Plans.WeightedAdjoint() it is the weighted adjoint a_ℓm = Σⱼ wⱼ fⱼ Y_ℓm(xⱼ) over harmonics of mean square 1. Synthesis is their direct sum at the points; neither forms the M × (lmax+1)² matrix of harmonics, so memory is O(M + (lmax+1)²).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.DirectSHTSphericalPlan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.sphere_coeffs","page":"API Reference","text":"sphere_coeffs(plan, field) -> C\n\nSpherical-harmonic analysis: the coefficients C of field up to the plan's band limit. For a structured grid this is the forward SHT; for scattered points it is the least-squares fit or the weighted adjoint. C is opaque and valid only with the interface functions of plan or of a plan of its kind at its band limit, such as sphere_apply!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.sphere_coeffs"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.sphere_coeffs!","page":"API Reference","text":"sphere_coeffs!(C, plan, field) -> C\n\nIn-place sphere_coeffs: analyse field into the pre-allocated coefficient container C, which must have come from sphere_coeffs_buffer(plan).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.sphere_coeffs!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.sphere_coeffs_buffer","page":"API Reference","text":"sphere_coeffs_buffer(plan) -> C\n\nA coefficient container of the right type and size for plan, suitable for sphere_coeffs!. Backends whose coefficients are not a plain vector (the FastSphericalHarmonics triangular layout, the NUFSHT dense spin layout) return their own shape.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.sphere_coeffs_buffer"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.sphere_field_buffer","page":"API Reference","text":"sphere_field_buffer(plan) -> field\n\nA grid container of the right type and size for plan, for a band synthesised at that plan's band limit. Defaults to sphere_coeffs_buffer, which is the same shape on backends whose coefficients and samples share a layout.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.sphere_field_buffer"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.sphere_apply!","page":"API Reference","text":"sphere_apply!(out, plan, C, h) -> out\n\nApply the per-degree multiplier h(ℓ) to (a copy of) the coefficients C and synthesise the result into out — i.e. out = Σ_{ℓm} h(ℓ) · C_{ℓm} · Y_{ℓm}. Does not mutate C.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.sphere_apply!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.sphere_mean","page":"API Reference","text":"sphere_mean(plan, field) -> scalar\n\nSpherical average of field under the plan's quadrature: for scattered points the sample mean, or the weighted mean when the plan carries weights; for a structured grid the exact quadrature integral. Provided by each backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.sphere_mean"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.sphere_plan_at","page":"API Reference","text":"sphere_plan_at(plan, lmax) -> plan or nothing\n\nA plan of the same kind at the lower band limit lmax, or nothing when this backend cannot be narrowed, in which case every band is synthesised at the full band limit. This is the spherical form of decimation: band-pass wavelet j is confined to degrees ≲ ℓ_j.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.sphere_plan_at"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.sphere_lmax","page":"API Reference","text":"sphere_lmax(plan) -> Int\n\nThe band limit of plan. Every backend that defines sphere_plan_at defines this.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.sphere_lmax"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.sphere_restrict!","page":"API Reference","text":"sphere_restrict!(Cdst, pdst, Csrc, psrc) -> Cdst\n\nCopy the spherical-harmonic coefficients of degree ≤ pdst's band limit out of Csrc (laid out for psrc) into Cdst (laid out for pdst), zeroing anything Cdst holds beyond them. Coefficient layout is backend-specific, so each backend defines this.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.sphere_restrict!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.grid_plan","page":"API Reference","text":"grid_plan(plan, L) -> plan or nothing\n\nA plan of plan's kind, band limit and batch width on the Gauss–Legendre grid of band limit L ≥ lmax (gauss_legendre_grid), analysing by that grid's quadrature, or nothing for a backend with none. Its coefficients are laid out as plan's. Its analysis of a field holding degrees up to 2L + 1 − lmax is exact. A scattered transform analyses its samples with plan and runs every later step of its cascade on this one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.grid_plan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.grid_band","page":"API Reference","text":"grid_band(grid) -> Int\n\nThe band limit L of a grid_plan's Gauss–Legendre grid, from its (L + 1)(2L + 1) nodes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.grid_band"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.coeffs_mean","page":"API Reference","text":"coeffs_mean(plan, C, b = 1)\n\nThe spherical mean of the field whose coefficients under plan are field b of C.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.coeffs_mean"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.gauss_legendre_grid","page":"API Reference","text":"gauss_legendre_grid(lmax, T) -> (θ, φ, w)\n\nThe Gauss–Legendre grid of band limit lmax, flattened with colatitude fastest: the lmax + 1 roots of P_{lmax+1}(cos θ) by 2lmax + 1 equispaced longitudes, and quadrature weights w summing to 1. The rule integrates the product of two harmonics of degree ≤ lmax exactly, so its weighted adjoint expands a degree-lmax field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.gauss_legendre_grid"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.batch_transform","page":"API Reference","text":"batch_transform(st, B) -> st\n\nst over B co-located fields per call, its plan and grid through batch_plan: each is st's own at its own width and otherwise built for the copy, which close_task_local! releases.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.batch_transform"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.task_local_batch","page":"API Reference","text":"task_local_batch(st, B) -> st\n\nA copy of st over B co-located fields per call, safe to run while st runs in another task: its plan and grid through task_local_batch_plan. Release it with close_task_local!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.task_local_batch"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.band_lmax","page":"API Reference","text":"band_lmax(lmax, J, j, headroom) -> Int\n\nBand limit that carries wavelet j: headroom · ℓ_j, clamped to lmax; headroom = Inf keeps lmax.\n\nBand j is a difference of Gaussians whose transfer above its cutoff is exp(-ℓ(ℓ+1) / 2ℓ_j(ℓ_j+1)): 0.135 at 2ℓ_j, 0.011 at 3ℓ_j, 3.4e-4 at 4ℓ_j, the largest value a given headroom discards.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.band_lmax"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.band_plans","page":"API Reference","text":"band_plans(plan, lmax, ls) -> Vector or nothing\n\nOne plan per scale, scale j at band limit ls[j], or nothing if this backend cannot narrow or no band needs narrowing. A scale at the full band limit lmax reuses plan.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.band_plans"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.SphericalScatteringTransform","page":"API Reference","text":"SphericalScatteringTransform\n\nSpherical scattering transform over a backend plan::P. sigma2[k+1] is the Gaussian-transfer variance for the dyadic low-pass cutoff ℓ_k (k=0..J); band-pass wavelet j is lowpass(ℓ_{j-1}) − lowpass(ℓ_j). bands holds one narrowed plan per scale (band_plans), or nothing. grid is the plan the cascade runs on after plan analyses the field (grid_plan), or nothing for a transform whose cascade runs on plan.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.SphericalScatteringTransform"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.SphericalMonogenicScatteringTransform","page":"API Reference","text":"SphericalMonogenicScatteringTransform\n\nSpherical monogenic scattering: shares the dyadic difference-of-Gaussians bands of SphericalScatteringTransform but replaces the analytic modulus with the spherical monogenic amplitude A_j = √(U⁰_j² + |∇_S g_j|²) (see monogenic_amplitude!). grid is as in SphericalScatteringTransform.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.SphericalMonogenicScatteringTransform"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.SphericalWorkspace","page":"API Reference","text":"SphericalWorkspace{A,C,AV,CV}\n\nScratch for one spherical cascade: the band-pass output band, the current first-order field u1, both on the samples the cascade runs on (the transform's grid, where it has one), and two coefficient containers, C for the input field and C1 for the first-order field being re-analysed. Two suffice, since the cascade finishes every child of a scale before starting the next. bband, bu1, bC and bC1 are the same per scale at its narrowed band limit, and empty when the transform has no bands.\n\nBuild one with SphericalWorkspace(st, field) and reuse it across calls; a task that transforms concurrently needs its own, with a task_local transform.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.SphericalWorkspace"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.SphericalMonogenicWorkspace","page":"API Reference","text":"SphericalMonogenicWorkspace{A,C,S}\n\nScratch for one spherical monogenic cascade: the current amplitude field u1, a second amp for the order-2 amplitudes, two coefficient containers, and scratch — the Riesz energy field and the plan's riesz_scratch (see monogenic_amplitude!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.SphericalMonogenicWorkspace"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.spherical_scattering!","page":"API Reference","text":"spherical_scattering!(S1, S2, st, ws, field) -> ScatteringCoefficients1D\n\nIn-place spherical scattering into pre-allocated S1/S2 using the workspace ws (see SphericalWorkspace). The returned container uses the supplied arrays.\n\nGrouped by first-order scale so only one first-order field and one coefficient vector are live at a time: scale j1 is band-passed, averaged into S1[j1], analysed once, and then consumed by every coarser j2 > j1 before the next j1 begins.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.spherical_scattering!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.spherical_scattering_batch!","page":"API Reference","text":"spherical_scattering_batch!(S1, S2, st, ws, X) -> (; S0, S1, S2)\n\nCascade over a stack of B fields sampled at the same points: X is (M, B), S1 is (J, B) and S2 is (J, J, B). Every transform covers all B fields in one call; st.plan, and its grid, must have batch width B (batch_transform).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.spherical_scattering_batch!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.spherical_monogenic_scattering!","page":"API Reference","text":"spherical_monogenic_scattering!(S1, S2, st, ws, field) -> ScatteringCoefficients1D\n\nIn-place spherical monogenic scattering, the monogenic counterpart of spherical_scattering!, grouped by first-order scale with one amplitude field live at a time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.spherical_monogenic_scattering!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.monogenic_amplitude!","page":"API Reference","text":"monogenic_amplitude!(amp, st, C, j, w) -> amp\n\nSpherical monogenic amplitude at scale j of the field whose (already-computed) SH coefficients are C, written into amp on the samples the cascade runs on. w holds the Riesz energy field r2 and that plan's riesz scratch.\n\nWith g_j = (−Δ_S)^{-1/2} U⁰_j the spin-1 Riesz field has |U^R_j| = |∇_S g_j|, so A_j = √(U⁰_j² + |∇_S g_j|²), the energy evaluated by sphere_riesz_energy!. The Riesz vector (orientation/phase) is spherical_monogenic_components.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.monogenic_amplitude!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.sphere_riesz_energy!","page":"API Reference","text":"sphere_riesz_energy!(out, plan, C, h, scratch) -> out\n\n|∇_S g|² at the plan's samples for g = Σ_{ℓm} h(ℓ)·C_{ℓm}·Y_{ℓm}, exact for coefficients of degree ≤ lmax. scratch comes from riesz_scratch. Does not mutate C.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.sphere_riesz_energy!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.riesz_scratch","page":"API Reference","text":"riesz_scratch(plan, field) -> scratch\n\nThe scratch sphere_riesz_energy! needs for plan and fields shaped like field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.riesz_scratch"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.sph_layout_riesz_energy!","page":"API Reference","text":"sph_layout_riesz_energy!(out, plan, C, h, lmax, scratch) -> out\n\nsphere_riesz_energy! for coefficients in the real sph_mode layout, by three scalar syntheses through sphere_apply!. With L = -i r×∇, a real g has |∇_S g|² = |L₊g|² + (∂_φ g)², and both operators act within each degree: L₊Y_{ℓm} = √((ℓ-m)(ℓ+m+1)) Y_{ℓ,m+1} on the Condon–Shortley complex harmonics, and ∂_φ maps cos(mφ) ↦ -m sin(mφ), sin(mφ) ↦ m cos(mφ). L₊g = P + iQ is synthesised as its real and imaginary parts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.sph_layout_riesz_energy!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.task_local","page":"API Reference","text":"task_local(st) -> st\n\nA copy of st safe to run concurrently with it: the plan's read-only tables are shared and its analysis and synthesis scratch duplicated by Plans.task_local_plan. Release it with close_task_local!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.task_local"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.close_task_local!","page":"API Reference","text":"close_task_local!(stl, st) -> nothing\n\nPlans.close_plan! every plan task_local(st), batch_transform or task_local_batch built for stl; plans stl shares with st stay open.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.close_task_local!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.band_multiplier","page":"API Reference","text":"Per-degree multiplier for the difference-of-Gaussians band-pass wavelet j.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.band_multiplier"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.dog_sigma2","page":"API Reference","text":"dog_sigma2(lmax, J, T) -> Vector{T}\n\nGaussian-transfer variances for cutoffs ℓ_k = lmax / 2^k, k=0..J. Band j is lowpass(ℓ_{j-1}) − lowpass(ℓ_j), with scale 1 finest.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.dog_sigma2"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.structured_grid","page":"API Reference","text":"structured_grid(lmax, T) -> (Θ, Φ)\n\nColatitudes Θ (length lmax+1) and longitudes Φ (length 2lmax+1) of the equiangular structured grid. Matches FastSphericalHarmonics.sph_points(lmax+1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.structured_grid"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.plan_points","page":"API Reference","text":"plan_points(plan) -> (θ, φ) or nothing\n\nThe sample locations a spherical plan was built on, from which a transform is rebuilt on another process, or nothing when the plan is defined by its band limit alone (a structured grid).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.plan_points"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.plan_weights","page":"API Reference","text":"plan_weights(plan) -> weights or nothing\n\nThe quadrature weights the plan averages with, or nothing for a backend that takes the unweighted sample mean or whose quadrature follows from its grid. A plan rebuilt on the same points needs these too: the in-core structured plan's Fejér weights are not uniform.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.plan_weights"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.plan_solver","page":"API Reference","text":"plan_solver(plan) -> (; rtol, maxiter, analysis)\n\nThe analysis policy and fit tolerances of a scattered-sphere plan. nothing for a plan whose analysis is an exact transform.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.plan_solver"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.batch_plan","page":"API Reference","text":"batch_plan(plan, B) -> plan or nothing\n\nAn equivalent plan that transforms B co-located fields per call, for spherical_scattering_batch!, or nothing if this backend has no batched form. Only the batch width differs from plan.\n\nReturns plan itself when Plans.batch_width(plan) == B, and otherwise a plan the caller owns and closes with Plans.close_plan!. Concurrent tasks use task_local_batch_plan.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.batch_plan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.task_local_batch_plan","page":"API Reference","text":"task_local_batch_plan(plan, B) -> plan or nothing\n\nA B-wide plan safe to apply while plan is applied in another task, owned by the caller, or nothing when the backend has no batched form.\n\nA spherical plan's analysis solves through its own scratch, so tasks cannot share one. At plan's own width this is Plans.task_local_plan(plan), and at any other width batch_plan builds a fresh one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.task_local_batch_plan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.supports_batch","page":"API Reference","text":"supports_batch(plan) -> Bool\n\nWhether batch_plan can widen this plan, answered without building one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.supports_batch"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.plan_nufft","page":"API Reference","text":"plan_nufft(plan) -> SpectralBackends.AbstractSpectralBackend\n\nThe NUFFT backend a scattered-sphere plan resolved to, or AutoSpectralBackend() for a plan that runs none. Plans.spectral_backend reports NUFSHTSpectralBackend whichever NUFFT runs underneath, so a transform rebuilt on another process needs this too to run the same one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.plan_nufft"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.plan_spin","page":"API Reference","text":"plan_spin(plan) -> (spin-0 plan, spin-1 plan) or nothing\n\nThe spin-weighted plan pair a scattered-sphere plan carries for spherical_monogenic_components, or nothing for a plan built without one. Only the monogenic constructors build it. The pair is working state, so concurrent use needs Plans.task_local_plan.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.plan_spin"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.default_rtol","page":"API Reference","text":"default_rtol(spectral) -> Real\n\nTolerance for the scattered-sphere analysis solve under spectral: 1e-8 for the in-core direct sum, which evaluates the harmonics exactly, and 1e-4 for NUFSHT, whose analysis is accurate only to its own approximation error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.default_rtol"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.make_spherical_plan","page":"API Reference","text":"make_spherical_plan(kind, spectral, θ, φ, lmax, T; rtol, maxiter, weights, ntrans, nufft, analysis)\n    -> AbstractSphericalPlan\n\nBuild the scattered-sphere plan selected by spectral over points (θ, φ) and band limit lmax. SpectralBackends.DirectSumSpectralBackend is the dependency-free default; SpectralBackends.NUFSHTSpectralBackend (or SpectralBackends.AutoSpectralBackend once the NUFSHT extension is loaded) uses the NUFSHT fast path.\n\nntrans = B builds a plan that transforms B co-located fields per call, and weights are the quadrature weights of the spherical mean. LeastSquares() fits coefficients and WeightedAdjoint() applies the weighted adjoint. kind = SphericalMonogenicScatteringTransform builds NUFSHT's spin pair for spherical_monogenic_components. The in-core plan takes the surface gradient of (−Δ_S)^{-1/2}U⁰ and accepts only an Auto nufft.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.make_spherical_plan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.make_structured_plan","page":"API Reference","text":"make_structured_plan(spectral, lmax, T; rtol, maxiter) -> AbstractSphericalPlan\n\nBuild the structured-sphere plan selected by spectral. SpectralBackends.DirectSumSpectralBackend is the dependency-free default (direct SHT on the grid); SpectralBackends.FSHTSpectralBackend (or SpectralBackends.AutoSpectralBackend once the FastSphericalHarmonics extension is loaded) uses the fast exact SHT.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.make_structured_plan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.nusht_spherical_plan","page":"API Reference","text":"nusht_spherical_plan(kind, θ, φ, lmax, T; rtol, maxiter, weights, ntrans, nufft, analysis)\n\nScattered-sphere plan on NUFSHT, a method the NUFSHT extension defines; this fallback throws an ArgumentError naming using NUFSHT.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.nusht_spherical_plan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.fsh_structured_plan","page":"API Reference","text":"fsh_structured_plan(lmax, T)\n\nStructured-sphere plan on FastSphericalHarmonics, a method the FastSphericalHarmonics extension defines; this fallback throws an ArgumentError naming using FastSphericalHarmonics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.SphericalCore.fsh_structured_plan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.plot_coefficients","page":"API Reference","text":"plot_coefficients(c; signal=nothing) — plot 1D scattering coefficients.\nplot_coefficients(c; image=nothing) — plot 2D scattering coefficients.\n\nPassing the input adds a panel showing it. Requires using CairoMakie.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.plot_coefficients"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ScatteringTransforms.plot_filter_bank","page":"API Reference","text":"plot_filter_bank(fb) — plot a 1D or 2D filter bank. Requires `using CairoMakie`.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ScatteringTransforms.plot_filter_bank"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Theory-of-Scattering-Transforms","page":"Theory","text":"The wavelet scattering transform builds translation-invariant, deformation-stable descriptors of signals, images, and volumes by cascading wavelet convolutions with a pointwise modulus. This page describes exactly what ScatteringTransforms.jl computes.","title":"Theory of Scattering Transforms"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Two-outputs:-averaged-coefficients-and-the-localized-field","page":"Theory","text":"For a signal x, define the propagated field along a path p = (λ₁, λ₂, …) of wavelet indices:\n\nU_0 x = xqquad U_px = bigU_p x star psi_lambda_kbig\n\nwhere p' drops the last index λ_k. The package exposes two reductions of U_p x:\n\nAveraged coefficients (st(x), the default) — the spatial mean of each propagated field,\nbar S_p x = biglangle U_p x bigrangle \nThese are the scalar \"scattering coefficients\" used as texture/field statistics (Cheng & Ménard 2021). Concretely S0 = ⟨x⟩, S1[λ] = ⟨|x ⋆ ψ_λ|⟩, and S2[λ₁,λ₂] = ⟨||x ⋆ ψ_{λ₁}| ⋆ ψ_{λ₂}|⟩.\nLocalized field (scattering_field(st, x)) — Mallat's translation-covariant field, low-pass filtered by the scaling function φ_J and subsampled,\nS_p x = big(U_p x star phi_Jbig)downarrow s \nThe spatial mean of S_p x equals \\bar S_p x, so the two outputs are consistent and the tests enforce that exactly. Under ↓ s this needs more of φ_J than \\hat\\phi_J(0)=1 — see The low-pass and decimation.\n\nAveraging over the domain, or low-pass filtering by φ_J, makes the descriptors invariant to translations; the localized field is also stable to small diffeomorphisms (Mallat 2012).","title":"Two outputs: averaged coefficients and the localized field"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Admissible-paths","page":"Theory","text":"Coefficients are only computed along paths whose effective scale is strictly increasing, j_eff(λ_{k+1}) > j_eff(λ_k) — each successive wavelet is strictly coarser (lower frequency). For 2D/3D this means the scale strictly increases while all orientation pairs are allowed; same-scale pairs are excluded. The admissible paths are enumerated once into a ScatteringTree.","title":"Admissible paths"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Wavelets","page":"Theory","text":"","title":"Wavelets"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#1D-Morlet","page":"Theory","text":"In the Fourier domain, normalized frequency ω ∈ [0, ½],\n\nhatpsi_j(omega) = e^-(omega-xi_j)^22sigma_j^2 - kappa_j e^-omega^22sigma_j^2\nqquad xi_j = tfrac12  2^-jQ\n\nwith the constant-Q bandwidth σ_j = ξ_j (1-2^{-1/Q})/(1+2^{-1/Q})/\\sqrt{2\\ln(1/r)}, which puts the crossing of adjacent wavelets at r of their peak, Q wavelets per octave. The second term enforces the zero-mean admissibility condition \\hat\\psi_j(0)=0. The filter is analytic (zero for ω<0).","title":"1D Morlet"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#2D-oriented-Morlet","page":"Theory","text":"At scale j and orientation θ = πℓ/L (ℓ = 0,…,L-1), with angular wavenumber k, k_∥ = k·\\hat θ and k_⊥ the component across it:\n\nhatpsi(k) = e^-((k_parallel-k_0)^2sigma_parallel^2 + k_perp^2sigma_perp^2)2\n            - beta e^-(k_parallel^2sigma_parallel^2 + k_perp^2sigma_perp^2)2\nqquad beta = e^-(k_0sigma_parallel)^22\n\nfor k_∥ ≥ 0 and zero otherwise, with real-space widths σ_∥ = 0.8·2^j, σ_⊥ = σ_∥ L/4 and k_0 = 3π/(4·2^j). The envelope's angular standard deviation at |k| = k_0 is 4/(L k_0σ_∥), a fixed fraction 0.68 of the orientation spacing π/L.","title":"2D oriented Morlet"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#3D-oriented-Morlet","page":"Theory","text":"The same envelope about a unit direction \\hat n, with k_∥ = k·\\hat n, over n_{orient} directions spread on the sphere. With their spacing Δθ = \\sqrt{4π/n_{orient}} in place of π/L, the 2D rule σ_⊥ = σ_∥ π/(4Δθ) gives σ_⊥ = σ_∥\\sqrt{π n_{orient}}/8.","title":"3D oriented Morlet"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Littlewood–Paley-normalization","page":"Theory","text":"For a real field the wavelet layer Wx = (x ⋆ φ_J,\\ x ⋆ ψ_λ)_λ satisfies\n\nWx^2 = frac1N sum_k A(k)hat x(k)^2qquad\nA(k) = hatφ_J(k)^2 + tfrac12 sum_λ big(hatψ_λ(k)^2 + hatψ_λ(-k)^2big)\n\nso (1-α)\\|x\\|^2 ≤ \\|Wx\\|^2 ≤ \\|x\\|^2 when 1-α ≤ A ≤ 1 (Andén & Mallat 2014). Every wavelet is scaled by one constant c that puts the maximum of the wavelet part of A at 1 for the bank continued over every scale. That bank is self-similar, so its A is periodic in log-frequency (and in angle) and c depends on the wavelet design alone: lp_scale_1d(Q), lp_scale_2d(L), lp_scale_3d(n_orient). A wavelet at scale j is then the same function in a bank of any depth J, and so is its coefficient.\n\nlittlewood_paley(fb) returns A on the bank's grid. \\hatφ_J(0) = 1 and \\hatψ_λ(0) = 0 give A(0) = 1. A is smallest in the band between φ_J and the coarsest wavelet, and in 2D/3D at the corners of the frequency grid, past the finest wavelet.\n\nOn an even axis the Nyquist bin holds ±½ at once. A wavelet's value there is the root mean square of its values at the two aliases, so A at that bin equals the continuous sum at ½.","title":"Littlewood–Paley normalization"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#The-low-pass-and-decimation","page":"Theory","text":"The coefficients \\bar S_p x = ⟨U_p x⟩ contain no low-pass: the average is over the whole domain. The localized field S_p x = U_p x \\star φ_J is defined by its low-pass, the Gaussian \\hatφ_J(k) = e^{-|k|^2σ^2/2} with σ = σ_0 2^J, σ_0 = 0.8 (ScatteringTransforms.Filters.gaussian_lowpass!), which is also every bank's averaging.\n\nSubsampling that field by s forces one condition. Since\n\nlangle S_p xrangle = tfrac1N sum_m big(hat U_phat φ_Jbig)m Ns\n\n⟨S_p x⟩ = \\bar S_p x iff \\hatφ_J vanishes on the subsampling lattice \\{mN/s : m ≠ 0\\}. The lattice starts at |k| = 2π/s, so at the default s = 2^{J-1} the largest term is e^{-(4πσ_0)^2/2}, 10^{-22} at σ_0 = 0.8.","title":"The low-pass and decimation"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Reduced-descriptors","page":"Theory","text":"For analysis it is common to reduce the raw coefficients (Reductions module, and compute_shape_sparsity for 2D):\n\nnormalized s1 = S1/S0, s2 = S2/S1 — remove dependence on overall amplitude;\nlog log S1, log S2 — gaussianize heavy-tailed coefficients of intermittent fields;\nsparsity s₂₁ = ⟨S₂/S₁⟩ over orientations — energy cascade from j₁ to coarser j₂;\nshape / anisotropy s₂₂ = ⟨S₂\\cos 2Δθ⟩/⟨S₂⟩ — the second angular harmonic, ≈ 0 for isotropic fields and nonzero for oriented structure.","title":"Reduced descriptors"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Reconstruction","page":"Theory","text":"There is no exact analytic inverse of the scattering transform — the modulus discards the local phase of each wavelet coefficient. Three reconstruction levels are available:\n\nExact linear wavelet-frame inverse (wavelet_transform / iwavelet). The complex, pre-modulus layer W_λ = x ⋆ ψ_λ with the low-pass Y = x ⋆ φ_J is invertible because A > 0, and the canonical dual frame gives, for a real field,\nx = mathrmRemathcal F^-1Bigbig(hatφ_Jhat Y + textstylesum_λ hatψ_λhat W_λbig)bigABig\nrecovered to machine precision (1D/2D/3D).\nPhase retrieval (reconstruct_phase) from the first-order moduli |x ⋆ ψ_λ| alone, via Gerchberg–Saxton alternating projections (reconstruct with the exact inverse, re-impose the target magnitudes, repeat); determined up to a global sign (Waldspurger & Mallat 2015).\nGradient-descent synthesis (synthesize, in the DifferentiationInterface extension) from the scattering coefficients themselves: from noise, minimize ‖S(\\hat x) − S(x)‖² (Bruna & Mallat microcanonical models). This yields a new sample with matching multiscale statistics — not the original field — and is differentiated through the mutation-free scattering(st, x) by any ADTypes backend (with Enzyme: AutoEnzyme(; mode = Enzyme.set_runtime_activity(Enzyme.Reverse))).","title":"Reconstruction"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Monogenic-(Riesz)-scattering","page":"Theory","text":"MonogenicScatteringTransform replaces the oriented analytic modulus with the rotation-covariant monogenic amplitude. From an isotropic band-pass ψ_j (radial in frequency, real, zero-mean) and the Riesz multipliers R_d(k) = -i\\,k_d/|k| (Σ_d|R_d|²=1 off the DC bin; R_d = 0 on axis d's Nyquist bin, the mean of its two aliases):\n\nA_j = sqrt(xstarpsi_j)^2 + textstylesum_d (xstar R_dpsi_j)^2\n\nwhich also yields a local phase and continuous orientation (monogenic_components). The band-pass and its Riesz components carry |\\hatψ_j|^2(1 + Σ_d|R_d|^2) per frequency, so A = |\\hatφ_J|^2 + Σ_j |\\hatψ_j|^2(1 + Σ_d|R_d|^2) and the wavelets are scaled by lp_scale_1d(Q)/2. On the sphere (spherical_monogenic_scattering) the Riesz operator R = ð∘(-Δ_S)^{-1/2} is harmonic-diagonal, and the Riesz energy is |U^R_j|² = |∇_S g_j|² with g_j = (-Δ_S)^{-1/2} of the band. With the angular momentum L = -i\\,r×∇, a real g has |∇_S g|^2 = |L_+ g|^2 + (∂_φ g)^2, and L_+ Y_{ℓm} = \\sqrt{(ℓ-m)(ℓ+m+1)}\\,Y_{ℓ,m+1} keeps the degree, so the energy is three scalar syntheses at the band limit, exact for band-limited g. The in-core direct plan takes the gradient from ∂_θ P̄_ℓ^m and ∂_φ evaluated at each point.","title":"Monogenic (Riesz) scattering"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Spherical-scale-convention","page":"Theory","text":"The low-pass cutoffs are ℓ_k = lmax / 2^k, k = 0,…,J. Band j is the difference G_{j-1}(ℓ) - G_j(ℓ), where G_k(ℓ) = exp(-ℓ(ℓ+1)/(2ℓ_k(ℓ_k+1))). Scale 1 is finest; the second-order cascade uses j2 > j1. Both spherical variants return ScatteringCoefficients1D with this ordering.","title":"Spherical scale convention"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Computation","page":"Theory","text":"Convolutions are done in the spectral domain. The core ships a dependency-free direct-sum DFT default; loading FFTW selects an O(N\\log N) fast path automatically (transform = AutoSpectralBackend()). Batches reuse one plan (scattering_batch), and using OhMyThreads enables a multithreaded batched transform (execution = ThreadedBackend()). The hot path is written with broadcasts/reductions so it also runs on GPU arrays. The mutation-free scattering(st, x) is the autodiff-friendly counterpart used by synthesis.","title":"Computation"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Applications","page":"Theory","text":"Texture and field classification, audio timbre, turbulence intermittency, and geophysical fields — settings where higher-order, non-Gaussian structure beyond the power spectrum is informative.","title":"Applications"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#References","page":"Theory","text":"Mallat, S. (2012). Group invariant scattering. Comm. Pure Appl. Math., 65(10), 1331–1398.\nBruna, J., & Mallat, S. (2013). Invariant scattering convolution networks. IEEE PAMI, 35(8), 1872–1886.\nAndén, J., & Mallat, S. (2014). Deep scattering spectrum. IEEE Trans. Signal Process.\nAllys, E. et al. (2019). The RWST, a comprehensive statistical description of the non-Gaussian structures in the ISM. A&A.\nCheng, T. Y., & Ménard, B. (2021). How to quantify fields or textures? A guide to the scattering transform. arXiv:2112.01288.\nWaldspurger, I., & Mallat, S. (2015). Phase retrieval for the Cauchy wavelet transform / wavelet transform modulus.\nBruna, J., & Mallat, S. (2018). Multiscale sparse microcanonical models. arXiv:1801.02013.\nFelsberg, M., & Sommer, G. (2001). The monogenic signal. IEEE Trans. Signal Process., 49(12).\nUnser, M., Sage, D., & Van De Ville, D. (2009). Multiresolution monogenic signal analysis using the Riesz–Laplace wavelet transform. IEEE Trans. Image Process., 18(11).","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"#ScatteringTransforms.jl","page":"Home","text":"A fast, generic Julia implementation of the wavelet scattering transform for 1D signals, 2D images, 3D volumes, and scalar fields on the sphere — with averaged and localized outputs, reduced descriptors, a dependency-free default plus pluggable fast backends, batching, threading, distributed/MPI, and GPU.","title":"ScatteringTransforms.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Why-scattering?","page":"Home","text":"Two signals can share an identical power spectrum yet differ structurally. Scattering distinguishes them: an intermittent signal has strong cross-scale coupling S₂/S₁, while its phase-randomized Gaussian surrogate does not — though their power spectra and first-order S₁ are the same.\n\n(Image: Discriminability)","title":"Why scattering?"},{"category":"section","location":"#Features","page":"Home","text":"Domains: 1D, 2D (oriented Morlet), 3D (oriented 3D Morlet), and spherical (S², smooth difference-of-Gaussians bands).\nGrid-support matrix: Cartesian × spherical, on uniform/structured and nonuniform/scattered sampling — gridded ScatteringTransform{D} (FFT), scattered-planar scattered_planar_scattering (exact direct NUDFT), structured-sphere structured_spherical_scattering (exact direct SHT), scattered-sphere spherical_scattering (exact direct SHT). Every cell has an in-core, dependency-free default; FFTW, NonuniformFFTs or FINUFFT, FastSphericalHarmonics and NUFSHT are optional fast paths selected via the transform keyword.\nTwo outputs: globally-averaged coefficients st(x) and the localized (Mallat) field scattering_field(st, x) = (|U_p x| ⋆ φ_J)↓ (their spatial means agree by construction).\nCorrect path structure: second order over strictly coarser scales, all orientation pairs.\nReduced descriptors: normalized_coefficients (S1/S0, S2/S1), log_coefficients, and 2D sparsity s₂₁ / anisotropy s₂₂ (compute_shape_sparsity).\nReconstruction: exact linear wavelet-frame inverse (wavelet_transform/iwavelet), phase retrieval (reconstruct_phase), and gradient-descent synthesize from coefficients (DifferentiationInterface extension, any ADTypes backend).\nMonogenic (Riesz) scattering: MonogenicScatteringTransform (1D/2D/3D) with the rotation-covariant monogenic amplitude + continuous orientation/phase (monogenic_components); on S², spherical_monogenic_scattering (amplitude) and spherical_monogenic_components (pointwise orientation/phase via the spin-1 Riesz vector).\nLittlewood–Paley normalized filter bank: 0 < A ≤ 1 with A = |φ̂|² + ½Σ_λ(|ψ̂_λ(k)|² + |ψ̂_λ(−k)|²), so the wavelet layer is non-expansive and invertible; a wavelet at scale j is the same function for every grid size and depth J.\nPluggable spectral backend: in-core direct-sum default; using FFTW → O(N log N) fast path (transform = AutoSpectralBackend() | DirectSumSpectralBackend() | FFTSpectralBackend()).\nScale: scattering_batch (one plan reused), ThreadedBackend (OhMyThreads), DistributedBackend/MPIBackend (parametric over the inner backend), GPUBackend (vendor-neutral KernelAbstractions: CUDA/ROCm/oneAPI/Metal, or KA.CPU()).\nGeneric & type-stable: Float32/Float64, autodiff-friendly, GPU-array-ready hot path.","title":"Features"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"Symbols live in submodules and are accessed via fully-qualified paths; the package does not re-export names into the top level (see the API Reference). Alias the package for brevity:\n\nusing ScatteringTransforms: ScatteringTransforms as ST\nusing FFTW: FFTW   # optional: loading it switches on the O(N log N) fast path automatically\n\n# 1D\nst = ST.ScatteringCore.ScatteringTransform(1024, 8; Q=1, max_order=2)\nc  = st(randn(1024))\nST.Coefficients.zeroth_order(c); ST.Coefficients.first_order(c); ST.Coefficients.second_order(c)\n\n# 2D / 3D\nc2 = ST.ScatteringCore.ScatteringTransform((256, 256), 4; L=8)(randn(256, 256))\nc3 = ST.ScatteringCore.ScatteringTransform((32, 32, 32), 3; n_orient=6)(randn(32, 32, 32))\n\n# localized field, reductions, batching\nfield = ST.ScatteringFields.scattering_field(st, randn(1024))   # per-path low-passed, subsampled maps\nred   = ST.Reductions.normalized_coefficients(c)                # s1 = S1/S0, s2 = S2/S1\nB     = ST.scattering_batch(st, randn(1024, 100))               # (coeffs × 100), one plan reused","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Filter-bank-and-its-Littlewood–Paley-sum","page":"Home","text":"(Image: Filter bank)","title":"Filter bank and its Littlewood–Paley sum"},{"category":"section","location":"#1D-and-2D-scattering","page":"Home","text":"(Image: 1D scattering) (Image: 2D scattering)","title":"1D and 2D scattering"},{"category":"section","location":"#Localized-field-and-reductions","page":"Home","text":"(Image: Localized field) (Image: Reductions)","title":"Localized field and reductions"},{"category":"section","location":"#Reconstruction-and-synthesis","page":"Home","text":"The complex (pre-modulus) wavelet layer is exactly invertible (iwavelet, machine precision); from the scattering coefficients, synthesize descends ‖S(x̂)−S(x)‖² (via DifferentiationInterface) to draw a new sample with matching multiscale statistics — in 1D and 2D.\n\nusing DifferentiationInterface: DifferentiationInterface\nusing Enzyme: Enzyme\nusing ADTypes: AutoEnzyme\n# exact linear inverse (machine precision)\nx̂ = ST.Inverse.iwavelet(st, ST.Inverse.wavelet_transform(st, signal))\n# coefficient synthesis from noise (a matching sample, not the original field). Runtime activity is\n# required: the cascade maps a closure over the filter bank, so every closure holds constant filters\n# next to the active input and Enzyme cannot separate the two statically.\nres = ST.synthesize(st, signal; iters = 400,\n                    backend = AutoEnzyme(; mode = Enzyme.set_runtime_activity(Enzyme.Reverse)))\n\nAny ADTypes backend works — the differentiable forward is the non-mutating scattering(st, x). Use the in-core direct-sum spectral backend for reverse mode: it is plain scalar arithmetic and needs no FFT rules.\n\n(Image: Reconstruction & synthesis (1D)) (Image: Reconstruction & synthesis (2D))","title":"Reconstruction & synthesis"},{"category":"section","location":"#Monogenic-(Riesz)-scattering","page":"Home","text":"MonogenicScatteringTransform uses the rotation-covariant monogenic amplitude in place of the oriented modulus, and monogenic_components recovers the local amplitude envelope and a continuous orientation (not quantized into bins).\n\n(Image: Monogenic analysis)","title":"Monogenic (Riesz) scattering"},{"category":"section","location":"#Nonuniform-/-scattered-planar-grids","page":"Home","text":"Off-lattice / gappy planar data is scattered onto a uniform Fourier mode grid by a nonuniform DFT (scattered_planar_scattering); on a uniform grid it reproduces the gridded FFT transform exactly. The caller gives the domain's size, period, which fixes every mode's wavelength, or a FlowGeometries grid that carries it. The default is an in-core exact direct NUDFT (no dependencies); using NonuniformFFTs or using FINUFFT enables the nonuniform FFT, and with both loaded transform = FlowTransformBindings.NonuniformFFTsBackend() or FlowTransformBindings.FINUFFTBackend() names one.\n\nanalysis = ST.Plans.WeightedAdjoint() is the planar default. Choose ST.Plans.LeastSquares() for a spectral fit, with rtol controlling its relative normal residual. tol sets NUFFT accuracy and nthreads sets the library thread count.\n\n(Image: Scattered planar scattering)","title":"Nonuniform / scattered planar grids"},{"category":"section","location":"#Spherical-scattering","page":"Home","text":"On S², both scattered points (spherical_scattering; in-core direct SHT by default, NUFSHT fast path when loaded) and a structured equiangular grid (structured_spherical_scattering, fast SHT) give matching coefficients; the monogenic Riesz energy |∇_S g|² is synthesised exactly from scalar transforms, and spherical_monogenic_components synthesizes the spin-1 Riesz vector for pointwise orientation/phase.\n\nSpherical coefficients follow the finest-first convention: scale 1 is finest and second-order pairs satisfy j2 > j1. The result is a ScatteringCoefficients1D. The scattered constructors accept spherical FlowGeometries grids and default to analysis = ST.Plans.LeastSquares().\n\n(Image: Spherical scattering) (Image: Structured spherical scattering) (Image: Spherical monogenic components)","title":"Spherical scattering"},{"category":"section","location":"#Backends-and-scale","page":"Home","text":"The in-core direct-sum transform is dependency-free; loading FFTW selects an O(N log N) fast path automatically (identical results). scattering_batch reuses one plan/workspace across a stack; using OhMyThreads, using Distributed, or using MPI enable scattering_batch(…; execution = ThreadedBackend()), DistributedBackend(…), and MPIBackend(…).\n\nOn the scattered surfaces the batch is also a transform axis: every scattered plan, the in-core direct sums included, transforms several co-located fields per call, so building with ntrans = B runs each cascade step as one transform over B fields.\n\nsp = ST.ScatteredPlanar.build(Float64, x, y, (32, 32), 3; period = 1.0, L = 4, ntrans = 8)\nST.scattering_batch(sp, X)                          # X is (M, B)\nST.scattering_batch(sp, X; execution = ThreadedBackend())   # composes with threads\n\nA plan's batch width is fixed when it is built, so such a transform runs a stack of any width 8 columns at a time, the last group padded with zero columns.\n\nTask workspaces and widened spherical plans are retained across calls. Changing a cached batch width releases its old plans; ST.close_transform!(st) releases retained plans, including plans held on Distributed workers. Each worker receives its assigned columns.\n\n(Image: Backend performance)","title":"Backends & scale"},{"category":"section","location":"#Documentation","page":"Home","text":"Theory — mathematical background\nAPI Reference — functions and types","title":"Documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citation","page":"Home","text":"@software{scatteringtransforms_jl,\n  author = {Benjamin, Jordan},\n  title = {ScatteringTransforms.jl: wavelet scattering in Julia},\n  url = {https://github.com/jbphyswx/ScatteringTransforms.jl}\n}","title":"Citation"}]
}
